#!/usr/bin/ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# admiral-eval — a BEHAVIORAL regression eval for the athena-admiral definition.
# It asserts that a post-shrink admiral still MAKES the right decision at a
# scripted fleet-state decision point — not merely that an invariant's words are
# still present somewhere (that is the faithfulness proxy T0/build-agents/
# check-agent-size already give). The gap this closes: a stage-4 trim can
# faithfully relocate an invariant's text into a JIT skill whose TRIGGER no
# longer fires — the words exist, the behavior is gone. See the design at
# ai/docs/admiral-eval-design.md.
#
# Two tiers, ONE runner, ONE baseline:
#   T1 (deterministic, no model) — hook-stdin cases: pipe a crafted PreToolUse
#     JSON into ai/hooks/<guard>.sh and assert the deny/allow, exactly like
#     harness-eval. Reused verbatim for the auth-hook deny (AE-13a).
#   T2 (model-in-loop) — present the admiral (the rendered definition named by
#     --agent-file, default this checkout's ai/agents/athena-admiral.md, passed
#     inline via `claude -p --agents` — see SUBJECT SELECTION at model_argv)
#     with ONE scripted scenario at a
#     decision point; it emits a structured decision trailer (NEXT_ACTIONS: /
#     labeled answer); a DETERMINISTIC scorer asserts the decision. There is NO
#     LLM-judge scoring tier — the model only PRODUCES a decision; the SCORING is
#     a deterministic predicate, so a red case is attributable to the admiral's
#     decision, not to a judge.
#
# HERMETICITY (design §2.1) — the eval must NEVER mutate real state. Two guards,
# both required and both proven in --self-test:
#   1. Plan-only framing (primary): every scenario ends with an explicit
#      instruction to output ONLY the decision trailer and call no tool / run no
#      command / spawn no agent. The admiral is scored on stated intent.
#   2. Structural tool containment via CLI flags (belt-and-suspenders —
#      structural for the three holes below, not just framing). The model runs
#      under the AMBIENT HOME (design §2.1 specified an
#      isolated HOME, but that is INFEASIBLE: `claude` resolves its auth from the
#      ambient HOME, so an isolated HOME yields no auth — verified empirically.
#      The agent definition no longer depends on HOME: since DND-503 the subject
#      is passed inline via --agents, never resolved from ~/.claude/agents). Dropping the isolated HOME re-opens the real forge/comms/
#      Agent surfaces, so containment is done with CLI flags that hold regardless
#      of the ambient settings' pre-approvals (see model_argv / CONTAINMENT_ARGV):
#        --strict-mcp-config --mcp-config {empty}  → NO MCP server loads, so the
#          Notion/Slack/Agent tools do not exist for the run (closes the corpus's
#          real temptations: AE-03's owner DM, AE-01/02's notion status writes).
#        --disallowedTools Bash Task               → no shell (which also closes
#          the absolute-path bypass of the PATH stubs — /usr/bin/gh, git push,
#          docker) and no real captain spawn.
#      A run therefore CANNOT reach MCP, CANNOT spawn a subagent, and CANNOT run
#      Bash, while auth + the inline --agents subject + the decision trailer still work
#      (all verified empirically). NOT structurally closed: the native Write/Edit
#      file tools remain available under the ambient HOME — a file mutation is
#      barred only by guard 1 (plan-only framing), not by these flags. It is out
#      of the three holes this hardening targets and, being a text-only decision
#      eval, no corpus case exercises it; noted so the containment is not read as
#      total. Guard 1 (plan-only framing) remains the primary framing; the inert PATH stubs (no-op gh/glab/gh-athena/glab-athena/docker +
#      a git wrapper that blocks `push`) remain as a third, residual layer for any
#      PATH-resolved call — now largely redundant since Bash itself is disallowed,
#      but kept as cheap defense in depth. Acceptable because `--run` is never in
#      the gate. (Hardening ratified by the coordinator post-EVAL-PR2.)
#
# Deliberately gem-free (stdlib only); run with bare `ruby`.
#
# Usage:
#   ai/bin/admiral-eval                 list fixtures + the trust model + how to run (no model)
#   ai/bin/admiral-eval --self-test     verify the scorer/parser/sampling/baseline/sandbox (no model; GATE-WIRED)
#   ai/bin/admiral-eval --run           execute cases (T1 deterministic; T2 model-in-loop), score, diff
#                                       vs baseline, exit 1 on regression  (MODEL IN LOOP; NOT in the gate)
#   ai/bin/admiral-eval --run --runs K          samples-per-T2-case (default 10; the row's PASS/FAIL is the
#                                       majority, the baseline diff is EvalScore.classify on the counts)
#   ai/bin/admiral-eval --run --update-baseline  rewrite ai/eval/admiral-baseline.json (deliberate only)
#   ai/bin/admiral-eval --run --only AE-05       run one case (local iteration)
#   ai/bin/admiral-eval --run --agent-file PATH  evaluate that rendered admiral
#                                       (default: this checkout's ai/agents/athena-admiral.md)
#   ai/bin/admiral-eval [--run] --agent captain|architect
#                                       the same runner, containment and scorer over another
#                                       agent's corpus (ai/eval/<agent>-fixtures), baseline and
#                                       action vocabulary (DND-529; registry: ai/lib/eval_subject.rb)
#
# Baseline PROVENANCE: the baseline records a fingerprint of the agent it was
# captured against (ai/agents/athena-admiral.md: line count + sha256), and every
# --run reports whether that subject still MATCHES, is STALE, or is UNKNOWN (a
# baseline written before fingerprinting). Without it, a baseline captured
# against a rewritten agent still prints "0 regressed" and reads exactly like a
# verified one — see report_provenance for the measured PR #32/#33 instance.
#
# Baseline CERTIFICATION (DND-522): the baseline also records the full corpus
# fingerprint, the runs it sampled, and each case's fixture fingerprint. Every
# diff ends in ONE `baseline verdict:` line — CERTIFIED only when the subject,
# the corpus and every case match; UNCERTIFIED (exit 0, each gap named, Fix:)
# on a subject or corpus mismatch, a case the baseline never measured, a
# changed fixture, or an --only run; FAILED (exit 1) on a regression, a
# baseline case the run lacks, or a failing case no baseline measurement
# covers. A present-but-unreadable baseline is an error, never "no baseline".
# --update-baseline refuses --only and --runs below the default N.
#
# Adding a case: create ai/eval/admiral-fixtures/<AE-NN-name>/meta plus scenario.md
# (T2) or an input.json (T1 hook-stdin). Keep the scorer deterministic — assert a
# parsed decision token / labeled answer / guard verdict, never a model judgement.

require "json"
require "open3"
require "digest"
require "fileutils"
require "tmpdir"
require "time"
require "rbconfig"
require "shellwords"
require_relative "../lib/strict_argv"

AI_DIR       = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)
REPO_ROOT    = File.expand_path("..", AI_DIR)
require File.join(AI_DIR, "lib", "eval_score")
require File.join(AI_DIR, "lib", "eval_subject")
HOOKS_DIR    = File.join(AI_DIR, "hooks")
# DND-523: the N the baseline diff's rule (EvalScore.classify) is calibrated at
# — variant-eval's DEFAULT_RUNS, adopted by DND-225. The self-test asserts the
# two stay equal. At K=3 a stochastic case like AE-18 (p≈0.78) majority-failed
# ~13% of runs with nothing changed.
DEFAULT_RUNS = 10

# DND-529: the subject is one of the agents EvalSubject::AGENTS assigns to this
# runner (admiral, captain, architect), chosen by --agent (default admiral).
# Its fixtures dir, baseline, render and inline subject key come from that one
# table, so each agent reuses this runner's containment and deterministic
# scorer instead of a forked copy. An agent the table does not assign here is a
# usage error, never zero cases.
RUNNER        = "ai/bin/admiral-eval"
DEFAULT_AGENT = "admiral"
SubjectError  = EvalSubject::SubjectError
MAX_SUBJECT_ARG_BYTES = EvalSubject::MAX_SUBJECT_ARG_BYTES
CONTAINMENT_ARGV      = EvalSubject::CONTAINMENT_ARGV

def eval_agent(key = DEFAULT_AGENT)
  EvalSubject.agent(key, runner: RUNNER)
end

def fixtures_dir(agent = eval_agent)
  File.expand_path(agent.fixtures_rel, REPO_ROOT)
end

def baseline_path(agent = eval_agent)
  File.expand_path(agent.baseline_rel, REPO_ROOT)
end

# The command a Fix: line tells the reader to re-run, for this agent.
def rerun_hint(agent = eval_agent)
  agent.key == DEFAULT_AGENT ? RUNNER : "#{RUNNER} --agent #{agent.key}"
end

# The render evaluated when --agent-file is omitted: THIS checkout's.
def default_agent_file(agent = eval_agent)
  File.join(REPO_ROOT, agent.render_rel)
end
# DND-537: the one range --runs must fall in, shared by the parser's
# validation and USAGE's rendered "1-99" so the two can never drift. Out of
# range is a usage error (exit 2), never a silent clamp — a clamped N would
# sample a different depth than requested with no sign anything changed.
RUNS_RANGE = (1..99).freeze

# The fixed action vocabulary shown to each agent in next-action scenarios. It
# is deliberately BROAD so its mere presence never reveals which invariant is
# under test (design §3.1). One list per agent this runner measures; the
# self-test fails any fixture that asserts a token its agent's list lacks (a
# forbidden token the model is never offered passes vacuously).
VOCABULARIES = {
  "admiral" => %w[
    confirm-merged notion.set-status:<value> slack.dm-owner agent.spawn-captain:<mission>
    merge-mr board-merge-train retarget-mr docker.compose-down invoke:teardown-worktree-stack
    invoke:epic-progress-dm sweep-fleet probe-worktree:<mission> list-agents
    wt-preflight:<branch> wait report-and-wait end-turn
    fleet-control.check mark-parked:<mission> sendmessage.park:<mission> sendmessage.main
    fleet-report.admiral-state:<state> invoke:admiral-final-report
    fleet-resume.status:<run> fleet-resume.drained:<run>
  ].freeze,
  "captain" => %w[
    read-design-docs probe-code:<path> write-plan write-test:<kind> run-test:<unfixed|fixed>
    apply-fix implement:<step> run-critic-review run-prep-commit commit push-branch open-mr
    watch-pipeline address-review merge-mr board-merge-train
    sendmessage.admiral sendmessage.architect sendmessage.main
    notion.set-status:<value> notion.comment:<ticket> file-ticket:<title>
    write-report wait end-turn
  ].freeze,
  "architect" => %w[
    ground:domain-model read-adrs kg.query notion.write-section:<section>
    notion.update-ticket-body:<ticket> notion.add-inline-pointer:<ticket> notion.create-ticket:<title>
    notion.mark-spec-ready:<ticket> questions-block:<topic> record-assumption:<topic>
    sendmessage.admiral sendmessage.captain:<ticket> sendmessage.main wait end-turn
  ].freeze
}.freeze

def vocabulary(agent = eval_agent)
  VOCABULARIES.fetch(agent.key)
end

# Appended to every T2 scenario. Plan-only framing (guard 1) + the required
# output shape the deterministic scorer parses.
def trailer_instructions(mode, vocab = vocabulary)
  common = <<~TXT
    ---
    This is an EVALUATION. Do NOT call any tool, do NOT run any command, do NOT
    spawn any agent, do NOT edit any file. Read the scenario and output ONLY the
    decision trailer specified below. You are scored on the next action(s) you
    DECLARE, not on execution.
  TXT
  case mode
  when "next-action"
    common + <<~TXT

      Output a single line naming the ordered action(s) you would perform NEXT,
      using ONLY tokens from this vocabulary (comma-separated, in order; extra
      prose above the line is ignored):

        #{vocab.join(' ')}

      NEXT_ACTIONS: <token>, <token>, ...
    TXT
  when "structured-answer"
    common + <<~TXT

      Answer the labeled question(s) the scenario asks with a single line each,
      of the form `LABEL: value` (e.g. `MODEL_CHOICE: opus`). Output only those
      labeled line(s); extra prose above them is ignored.
    TXT
  else
    common
  end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# meta parsing — shared key=value reader; REPEATABLE keys accumulate into a list
# (expect_action, forbid_action, expect_order can each appear many times).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
REPEATABLE = %w[expect_action forbid_action expect_order].freeze

def parse_meta(path)
  m = {}
  File.foreach(path) do |line|
    line = line.strip
    next if line.empty? || line.start_with?("#")
    k, _, v = line.partition("=")
    k = k.strip
    v = v.strip
    if REPEATABLE.include?(k)
      (m[k] ||= []) << v
    else
      m[k] = v
    end
  end
  m
end

def fixtures(agent = eval_agent)
  Dir.glob(File.join(fixtures_dir(agent), "*")).select { |d| File.file?(File.join(d, "meta")) }.sort
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trailer parsing — extract the ordered NEXT_ACTIONS token list and the labeled
# answer lines from the admiral's output. A garbled/absent trailer parses to
# empty; scoring treats a missing decision as a FAIL (never a pass).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def parse_trailer(text)
  actions = []
  na = text.to_s.lines.reverse.find { |l| l =~ /^\s*NEXT_ACTIONS\s*:/i }
  if na
    body = na.sub(/^\s*NEXT_ACTIONS\s*:/i, "").strip
    actions = body.split(",").map(&:strip).reject(&:empty?) unless body.casecmp?("none")
  end
  answers = {}
  text.to_s.lines.each do |l|
    if (mm = l.match(/^\s*([A-Z][A-Z0-9_]+)\s*:\s*(.+?)\s*$/))
      answers[mm[1]] = mm[2] # last occurrence wins
    end
  end
  { actions: actions, answers: answers }
end

# Does an expected/forbidden spec match an emitted action token?
# head must match (case-insensitive); if the spec carries an arg (`head:arg`),
# the token's arg must match too — a bare spec matches any arg.
def action_matches?(spec, token)
  s_head, _, s_arg = spec.partition(":")
  t_head, _, t_arg = token.partition(":")
  return false unless s_head.casecmp?(t_head)
  s_arg.empty? || s_arg.casecmp?(t_arg)
end

def norm(s)
  s.to_s.strip.downcase
end

# Which assertion keys a case actually carries (non-empty).
def assertion_keys(meta)
  keys = []
  keys << "expect_action" unless Array(meta["expect_action"]).empty?
  keys << "forbid_action" unless Array(meta["forbid_action"]).empty?
  keys << "expect_order"  unless Array(meta["expect_order"]).empty?
  keys << "answer_field"  if meta["answer_field"]
  keys
end

# Validate a case CONFIG (independent of any model output). Returns a list of
# reasons; empty means the config is well-formed. A case that asserts NOTHING,
# or a structured-answer case with no answer_field, would pass vacuously on an
# empty/garbled model response — that is a scorer that can't distinguish a
# correct decision from no decision, so it is rejected as a FAIL, never run.
def validate_case(meta)
  reasons = []
  mode = meta["mode"]

  if assertion_keys(meta).empty?
    reasons << "case asserts nothing — needs at least one of expect_action / " \
               "forbid_action / expect_order / answer_field, or an empty/garbled " \
               "model output would pass vacuously. Fix: add a concrete assertion to meta."
  end

  if mode == "structured-answer"
    unless meta["answer_field"]
      reasons << "structured-answer requires answer_field — a forbid_action-only " \
                 "structured case passes vacuously on empty output. Fix: add " \
                 "`answer_field = <LABEL>` and `expect_answer = <value>` (use mode " \
                 "next-action if the assertion is really over the action list)."
    end
    if meta["answer_field"] && !meta["expect_answer"]
      reasons << "answer_field #{meta['answer_field'].inspect} has no expect_answer. " \
                 "Fix: add `expect_answer = <value>`."
    end
  end

  # expect_order shape: `A < B`, a non-empty token on each side. A malformed
  # spec (no `<`) must FAIL its case with a Fix:, never raise and abort --run.
  Array(meta["expect_order"]).each do |spec|
    parts = spec.include?("<") ? spec.split("<", 2).map(&:strip) : []
    if parts.size != 2 || parts.any?(&:empty?)
      reasons << "malformed expect_order #{spec.inspect} — must be `A < B` with a " \
                 "token on each side. Fix: separate the two action tokens with `<`."
    end
  end

  reasons
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scorer — deterministic predicate over (meta, parsed decision).
# Returns [pass?, reasons]. Reasons are for the human/LLM reader on a fail.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def score_decision(meta, parsed)
  # A malformed case CONFIG is a FAIL, not a pass and not a crash (design: a
  # case must assert something; a broken spec must not take down the run).
  config_errs = validate_case(meta)
  return [false, config_errs.map { |r| "invalid case config: #{r}" }] unless config_errs.empty?

  reasons = []
  mode = meta["mode"]

  # A missing decision is never a pass (design §8).
  if mode == "next-action" && parsed[:actions].empty?
    return [false, ["no NEXT_ACTIONS trailer emitted (missing decision is a fail, not a pass)"]]
  end
  if mode == "structured-answer" && meta["answer_field"] && !parsed[:answers].key?(meta["answer_field"])
    return [false, ["no `#{meta['answer_field']}:` answer line emitted (missing decision is a fail)"]]
  end

  Array(meta["expect_action"]).each do |a|
    reasons << "expected action #{a.inspect} not in #{parsed[:actions].inspect}" \
      unless parsed[:actions].any? { |t| action_matches?(a, t) }
  end

  Array(meta["forbid_action"]).each do |a|
    reasons << "forbidden action #{a.inspect} present in #{parsed[:actions].inspect}" \
      if parsed[:actions].any? { |t| action_matches?(a, t) }
  end

  Array(meta["expect_order"]).each do |spec|
    a, b = spec.split("<", 2).map(&:strip)
    ia = parsed[:actions].index { |t| action_matches?(a, t) }
    ib = parsed[:actions].index { |t| action_matches?(b, t) }
    if ia.nil? || ib.nil?
      reasons << "order #{spec.inspect}: both must be present (#{a}=#{ia.inspect}, #{b}=#{ib.inspect})"
    elsif ia >= ib
      reasons << "order #{spec.inspect} violated: #{a}@#{ia} must precede #{b}@#{ib}"
    end
  end

  if meta["answer_field"]
    got = parsed[:answers][meta["answer_field"]]
    want = meta["expect_answer"]
    reasons << "answer #{meta['answer_field']}=#{got.inspect} != expected #{want.inspect}" \
      unless got && want && norm(got) == norm(want)
  end

  [reasons.empty?, reasons]
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Corpus lint (DND-529, model-free, run by --self-test over EVERY agent this
# runner measures). A corpus can be well-formed per case and still score
# nothing real:
#   - a case with no `invariant =` line cannot say which rule a red protects;
#   - a token its agent's vocabulary never offers can never be emitted, so a
#     forbid_action on it passes vacuously and an expect_action on it can never
#     pass;
#   - a case whose `examples` file (one `pass | <trailer>` or `fail |
#     <trailer>` per line) disagrees with the scorer asserts something other
#     than what its author believed.
# `examples` is the per-case scorer fixture: required for every agent but the
# admiral, whose corpus predates it (checked there when present).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
T2_MODES = %w[next-action structured-answer].freeze
KNOWN_MODES = (T2_MODES + %w[hook-stdin]).freeze

def spec_heads(meta)
  specs = Array(meta["expect_action"]) + Array(meta["forbid_action"]) +
          Array(meta["expect_order"]).flat_map { |s| s.split("<", 2).map(&:strip) }
  specs.reject(&:empty?).map { |s| s.partition(":").first }
end

# -> [[example_line, why], ...] for every example the scorer disagrees with.
def example_problems(dir, meta)
  path = File.join(dir, "examples")
  return [] unless File.file?(path)

  File.readlines(path, chomp: true).reject { |l| l.strip.empty? || l.start_with?("#") }.filter_map do |line|
    want, sep, trailer = line.partition("|")
    want = want.strip
    next [line, "not `pass | <trailer>` or `fail | <trailer>`"] if sep.empty? || !%w[pass fail].include?(want)

    got = score_decision(meta, parse_trailer(trailer.strip))[0]
    next nil if got == (want == "pass")

    [line, "labeled #{want} but the scorer says #{got ? 'pass' : 'fail'}"]
  end
end

# -> list of problem strings for one agent's corpus (empty = clean).
def corpus_problems(agent)
  dirs = fixtures(agent)
  return ["#{agent.fixtures_rel} has no case (zero cases is not a corpus)"] if dirs.empty?

  heads = vocabulary(agent).map { |t| t.partition(":").first }
  problems = []
  t2 = 0
  dirs.each do |d|
    name = File.basename(d)
    meta = parse_meta(File.join(d, "meta"))
    mode = meta["mode"]
    problems << "#{name}: unknown mode #{mode.inspect}" unless KNOWN_MODES.include?(mode)
    problems << "#{name}: no `invariant =` line naming the rule it protects" if meta["invariant"].to_s.strip.empty?
    next unless T2_MODES.include?(mode)

    t2 += 1
    problems << "#{name}: no scenario.md" unless File.file?(File.join(d, "scenario.md"))
    validate_case(meta).each { |r| problems << "#{name}: #{r}" }
    (spec_heads(meta) - heads).uniq.each do |h|
      problems << "#{name}: asserts token #{h.inspect}, which the #{agent.key} vocabulary never offers"
    end
    if agent.key != DEFAULT_AGENT
      ex = File.readlines(File.join(d, "examples"), chomp: true) rescue []
      problems << "#{name}: examples needs at least one `pass |` and one `fail |` line" \
        unless ex.any? { |l| l.start_with?("pass") } && ex.any? { |l| l.start_with?("fail") }
    end
    example_problems(d, meta).each { |line, why| problems << "#{name}: example #{line.inspect} #{why}" }
  end
  problems << "#{agent.fixtures_rel} has no T2 case" if t2.zero?
  problems
end

# strict majority true (design §4.3)
def majority(bools)
  bools.count(true) * 2 > bools.size
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# T1 hook-stdin primitive — reused verbatim from harness-eval. Isolated HOME so
# per-invocation hook dedup state can never make a "fires" case fire only once.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Returns [verdict, detail]. verdict is one of:
#   :fires — the hook RAN and emitted a PreToolUse deny.
#   :clean — the hook RAN and did NOT deny.
#   :error — the hook was missing / not executable, its input was unreadable, it
#            raised, or it did not exit normally (was signaled).
# :error is DISTINCT from :clean on purpose. A broken or absent hook returning
# "no deny" is NOT the same as a working hook that correctly allowed — collapsing
# them lets a `expect=clean` case pass while its guard is silently dead (the
# "failed lookup must not look like an empty one" class applied to the scorer
# itself). hook_pass? therefore never treats :error as a pass, for either
# expectation.
def hook_verdict(guard, input_path)
  hook_verdict_at(File.join(HOOKS_DIR, "#{guard}.sh"), input_path)
end

# Same three-state logic against an explicit hook path (used by --self-test to
# exercise the :fires / :clean / :error branches on stub hooks, so the real T1
# path and the self-test share one implementation).
def hook_verdict_at(hook, input_path)
  return [:error, "hook not found or not executable: #{hook}"] unless File.executable?(hook)
  return [:error, "input file not readable: #{input_path}"] unless File.file?(input_path)

  input = File.read(input_path)
  Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-home-") do |home|
    out, _err, st = Open3.capture3({ "HOME" => home }, hook, stdin_data: input)
    return [:error, "hook did not exit normally (signaled)"] unless st&.exited?
    out.include?('"permissionDecision":"deny"') ? [:fires, out] : [:clean, out]
  end
rescue StandardError => e
  [:error, "hook error: #{e.message}"]
end

# Pure verdict->pass mapping for a hook-stdin case. :error is NEVER a pass; only
# a matching ran-verdict passes. `expect` must be "fires" or "clean".
def hook_pass?(verdict, expect)
  case verdict
  when :fires then expect == "fires"
  when :clean then expect == "clean"
  else false # :error (or any unexpected verdict) can never satisfy a case
  end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inert PATH sandbox (guard 2, defense in depth). build_sandbox writes the no-op
# stubs into <dir>/bin and returns that bin dir. model_env front-loads it on PATH
# but does NOT override HOME — see the DEVIATION note in the file header:
# `claude` needs the ambient HOME to authenticate, so an isolated HOME is
# infeasible for the model run. (The subject agent is passed inline; see
# SUBJECT SELECTION at model_argv.)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
INERT_STUBS = %w[gh glab gh-athena glab-athena docker].freeze
INERT_EXIT  = 97

def build_sandbox(dir)
  bin = File.join(dir, "bin")
  FileUtils.mkdir_p(bin)

  INERT_STUBS.each do |name|
    path = File.join(bin, name)
    File.write(path, <<~SH)
      #!/bin/sh
      echo "admiral-eval: INERT STUB '#{name}' — this PATH-resolved forge/comms/container call was neutralized by the eval sandbox. No real action taken. Args: $*" >&2
      exit #{INERT_EXIT}
    SH
    FileUtils.chmod(0o755, path)
  end

  # git: forward reads to the real git, but BLOCK `push` (the one git mutation
  # that could escape the sandbox). Scans only the first positional token so a
  # commit message containing "push" is not blocked.
  real_git = which("git") || "/usr/bin/git"
  gitpath = File.join(bin, "git")
  # NOTE: do NOT `shift` here — `for a in "$@"` iterates a snapshot, but `shift`
  # mutates "$@", so the trailing `exec real_git "$@"` would forward corrupted
  # args. Find the first positional (the git subcommand) non-destructively.
  File.write(gitpath, <<~SH)
    #!/bin/sh
    sub=""
    skip=0
    for a in "$@"; do
      if [ "$skip" = 1 ]; then skip=0; continue; fi
      case "$a" in
        -C|-c) skip=1; continue;;
        -*) continue;;
        *) sub="$a"; break;;
      esac
    done
    if [ "$sub" = "push" ]; then
      echo "admiral-eval: INERT STUB 'git push' blocked in the eval sandbox. No real action taken." >&2
      exit #{INERT_EXIT}
    fi
    # Mark this shim as passed through before handing off (DND-1103). In an
    # agent session real_git is the agent PATH wrapper; a wrapper that predates
    # DND-1103 execs the first OTHER git on PATH, which is this shim again: an
    # exec loop that ends at ARG_MAX. Every wrapper skips a git named in
    # ATHENA_AGENT_GIT_SEEN. PATH is left whole, so git's own children (a `!`
    # alias, a hook) still resolve `git` and the other stubs to this sandbox.
    ATHENA_AGENT_GIT_SEEN="${ATHENA_AGENT_GIT_SEEN:+$ATHENA_AGENT_GIT_SEEN:}"#{Shellwords.escape(gitpath)}
    export ATHENA_AGENT_GIT_SEEN
    exec #{real_git} "$@"
  SH
  FileUtils.chmod(0o755, gitpath)

  bin
end

def which(cmd)
  ENV.fetch("PATH", "").split(File::PATH_SEPARATOR).each do |d|
    p = File.join(d, cmd)
    return p if File.executable?(p) && !File.directory?(p)
  end
  nil
end

# The env the model child runs under: the stub bin prepended to PATH. HOME is
# NOT overridden — it inherits the ambient HOME so `claude` can authenticate
# (see the DEVIATION note in the file header).
def model_env(bin)
  { "PATH" => "#{bin}:#{ENV['PATH']}" }
end

# Structural tool containment (guard 2, hardened — design §2.1) and SUBJECT
# SELECTION (DND-503) live in ai/lib/eval_subject.rb, shared with critic-eval
# (DND-529): every agent this runner measures gets the SAME argv shape. In
# brief: under the ambient HOME (required, see model_env) the model could
# otherwise reach real MCP servers, spawn a real captain via Task, and run
# arbitrary Bash, so the argv loads NO MCP server (--strict-mcp-config with an
# empty set) and disallows Bash + Task, LAST because --disallowedTools is
# variadic. And `claude --agent <name>` resolves <name> from ~/.claude/agents
# (the MAIN checkout) whatever the cwd, so the argv instead carries the NAMED
# render's prose inline, under `<name>-eval-subject`, a key no agents dir
# supplies: a lost --agents fails "agent not found" instead of silently
# evaluating main's copy. VERIFIED 2026-09-24 (claude 2.1.281) for the admiral.

# Parse a render into an --agents definition. Raises SubjectError.
def agent_definition(path, agent = eval_agent)
  EvalSubject.definition(path, agent.name)
end

# --agent-file PATH selects the subject; the default is THIS checkout's render
# of the selected agent (validated at the same model_argv call in run_model,
# before any model call), the same file subject_fingerprint records. Returns
# [absolute_path, nil] or [nil, error_message_with_Fix].
def resolve_agent_file(argv, agent = eval_agent)
  i = argv.index("--agent-file")
  return [default_agent_file(agent), nil] unless i

  raw = argv[i + 1]
  if raw.nil? || raw.start_with?("--")
    return [nil, "--agent-file needs a path. Fix: pass `--agent-file <path-to-rendered-#{agent.name}.md>`, " \
                 "or omit it to evaluate this checkout's #{relpath(default_agent_file(agent))}."]
  end

  path = File.expand_path(raw)
  model_argv(path, agent) # loads AND size-checks the subject, so every SubjectError surfaces here
  [path, nil]
rescue SubjectError => e
  [nil, e.message]
end

# DND-524: --output-format json is what makes the usage/cost fields exist on
# stdout at all — plain `-p` prints only the final assistant text. Spliced in
# BEFORE EvalSubject::CONTAINMENT_ARGV's --strict-mcp-config, never appended
# after it (--disallowedTools is variadic and would swallow it). This stays
# LOCAL to admiral-eval, never inside EvalSubject.argv: critic-eval builds its
# own argv straight from EvalSubject.argv and its run_model expects plain
# text, not a JSON envelope.
def model_argv(agent_file, agent = eval_agent)
  argv = EvalSubject.argv(agent_file, agent)
  at = argv.index("--strict-mcp-config") || argv.size
  argv[0...at] + ["--output-format", "json"] + argv[at..]
end

# DND-524: pull the usage/cost fields out of a `claude -p --output-format
# json` result, or nil when the run reported none. nil (not a zero-filled
# hash) is what lets usage_summary_line tell "nothing measured" from "measured
# a zero-token call" apart.
def extract_usage(parsed)
  usage = parsed["usage"]
  return nil unless usage.is_a?(Hash)

  {
    "input_tokens" => usage["input_tokens"].to_i,
    "output_tokens" => usage["output_tokens"].to_i,
    "cache_read_input_tokens" => usage["cache_read_input_tokens"].to_i,
    "cache_creation_input_tokens" => usage["cache_creation_input_tokens"].to_i,
    "cost_usd" => parsed["total_cost_usd"].to_f
  }
end

# Invoke the subject once for a T2 fixture, return its raw output (or nil on
# failure). The scenario body + the mode's trailer instructions form the
# prompt. usage_log is mutated with one entry per call: the extracted usage
# hash, or nil when the call failed, could not be parsed, or reported no usage
# (DND-524) — never a zero, so run_model's aggregate can tell "no calls
# measured" from "measured zero tokens". The stderr line keeps its
# `! <key> invocation failed` shape: for the admiral that is byte-identical to
# what variant-eval's T2_INVOCATION_FAILED parses.
def invoke_subject(scenario, mode, bin, argv, agent = eval_agent, usage_log = [])
  prompt = "#{scenario}\n\n#{trailer_instructions(mode, vocabulary(agent))}"
  out, err, st = Open3.capture3(model_env(bin), *argv, stdin_data: prompt)
  unless st&.success?
    warn "  ! #{agent.key} invocation failed (exit #{st ? st.exitstatus : '?'}): #{err.to_s.lines.last(2).join.strip}"
    usage_log << nil
    return nil
  end

  parsed = JSON.parse(out)
  usage_log << extract_usage(parsed)
  text = parsed["result"]
  # A missing `result` field is a CALL failure (the --output-format json
  # envelope didn't carry the text this scorer needs), never the subject's own
  # answer — it must read as "invocation failed" so variant-eval's
  # T2_INVOCATION_FAILED (`/^\s*! admiral invocation failed/`) counts it as a
  # failed call, not a scored FAIL misattributed to #{agent.key} (critic
  # finding on ffd8309).
  warn "  ! #{agent.key} invocation failed: --output-format json envelope had no `result` text " \
       "(raw, first 200 bytes): #{out.to_s[0, 200]}" if text.nil?
  text
rescue JSON::ParserError => e
  warn "  ! #{agent.key} invocation failed: could not parse --output-format json stdout (#{e.message}); " \
       "raw (first 200 bytes): #{out.to_s[0, 200]}"
  usage_log << nil
  nil
rescue StandardError => e
  warn "  ! #{agent.key} invocation failed: #{e.message} (is `claude` on PATH?)"
  usage_log << nil
  nil
end

# DND-524: one aggregate line over every T2 model call this run made, printed
# to STDERR ONLY (never stdout — variant-eval's collect_t2_counts,
# block-optimize, and `--json` parse admiral-eval's stdout byte-for-byte: the
# `subject:` line, the `PASS|FAIL ... [<mode>]` rows, and the closing
# `admiral-eval: P/T cases pass` summary; see the note at run_model's per-case
# progress line). Absence must never read as zero: "n/a" when nothing in the
# log carried usage (every call failed, or ran against a `claude` too old to
# emit `usage` under --output-format json), not a spend of $0.00.
def usage_summary_line(log)
  measured = log.compact
  return "admiral-eval: usage — n/a (no model call in this run reported usage tokens)" if measured.empty?

  sum = ->(key) { measured.sum { |u| u[key] } }
  cost = measured.sum { |u| u["cost_usd"] }
  "admiral-eval: usage — #{measured.size}/#{log.size} model call(s) reported usage; tokens: " \
    "input #{sum.('input_tokens')}, output #{sum.('output_tokens')}, " \
    "cache_read #{sum.('cache_read_input_tokens')}, cache_creation #{sum.('cache_creation_input_tokens')}; " \
    "cost $#{format('%.4f', cost)} USD"
end
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Evaluate one fixture directory. T1 runs once (deterministic); T2 samples K
# times and scores by majority. Returns {name, mode, pass, rate, detail}.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def evaluate(dir, runs:, argv:, agent: eval_agent, usage_log: [])
  meta = parse_meta(File.join(dir, "meta"))
  name = File.basename(dir)
  mode = meta["mode"]

  case mode
  when "hook-stdin"
    expect = meta["expect"]
    unless %w[fires clean].include?(expect)
      return { name: name, mode: mode, pass: false, rate: "0/0",
               detail: "invalid expect=#{expect.inspect} (must be fires|clean). Fix: set expect in meta." }
    end
    verdict, detail = hook_verdict(meta["guard"], File.join(dir, meta["input"].to_s))
    pass = hook_pass?(verdict, expect)
    { name: name, mode: mode, pass: pass, rate: "1/1",
      detail: "guard=#{meta['guard']} expect=#{expect} got=#{verdict} #{detail.to_s[0, 120]}" }
  when "next-action", "structured-answer"
    # Reject a malformed/vacuous case CONFIG BEFORE spending any model calls on
    # it — a case that asserts nothing (or a structured-answer with no
    # answer_field) must FAIL loudly, not pass vacuously on empty output.
    config_errs = validate_case(meta)
    unless config_errs.empty?
      # Nothing was sampled, so the rate is 0/0 (n/a), not 0/K (DND-225).
      return { name: name, mode: mode, pass: false, rate: "0/0",
               detail: "invalid case config: #{config_errs.join('; ')}" }
    end
    scenario = File.read(File.join(dir, "scenario.md"))
    Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-sandbox-") do |sb|
      bin = build_sandbox(sb)
      last_reasons = nil
      samples = Array.new(runs) do
        out = invoke_subject(scenario, mode, bin, argv, agent, usage_log)
        if out.nil?
          last_reasons ||= ["#{agent.key} produced no output (invocation failed)"]
          next false
        end
        ok, reasons = score_decision(meta, parse_trailer(out))
        last_reasons = reasons unless ok
        ok
      end
      passes = samples.count(true)
      pass = majority(samples)
      detail = pass ? "invariant=#{meta['invariant']}" : (last_reasons || []).join("; ")[0, 200]
      { name: name, mode: mode, pass: pass, rate: "#{passes}/#{samples.size}", detail: detail }
    end
  else
    { name: name, mode: mode, pass: false, rate: "0/0",
      detail: "unknown mode: #{mode.inspect}. Fix: set mode to hook-stdin|next-action|structured-answer." }
  end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# baseline load / diff. A case the baseline measured that this run measures
# significantly WORSE is the FAIL signal; an improvement is reported, never
# failed. --update-baseline is never used to hide a regression.
#
# DND-523: a T2 case is compared on its COUNTS with EvalScore.classify, the
# rule variant-eval uses (DND-225): regression = the Newcombe 95% interval of
# p_now - p_base lies below 0 OR the drop exceeds the 0.3 pilot envelope; a
# case the baseline measured but this run did not is :missing, a regression.
# It replaced a diff of K-sample majority booleans, which read a stochastic
# case (AE-18, p≈0.78) baselined as a pass as "pass -> fail" on ~13% of K=3
# runs with nothing changed, and which MISSED a real 10/10 -> 6/10 drop because
# 6/10 is still a majority pass. The rule is calibrated at N = DEFAULT_RUNS
# (variant-eval's N) per side. T1 (hook-stdin) cases are deterministic and
# always record rate 1/1, so they diff EXACTLY on pass, never through classify.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# fixture: the case's fixture fingerprint when the baseline was captured (nil in
# a baseline written before DND-522).
BaselineCase = Struct.new(:pass, :counts, :rate_error, :fixture)

# A baseline file that EXISTS but cannot be read is an error, never "no baseline
# committed yet" (DND-522): the old loader turned it into a Note and ran as if
# there were no baseline, so a corrupt committed baseline read like a clean
# first run.
class BaselineUnreadable < StandardError; end

# The parsed baseline document, nil when no baseline file exists, or raises
# BaselineUnreadable (with a Fix:) when one exists and cannot be used.
def load_baseline_doc(path = baseline_path)
  return nil unless path && File.exist?(path)

  data = JSON.parse(File.read(path))
  return data if data.is_a?(Hash) && data["cases"].is_a?(Array)

  raise BaselineUnreadable, baseline_unreadable_message(path, "it has no \"cases\" array")
rescue JSON::ParserError, SystemCallError => e
  raise BaselineUnreadable, baseline_unreadable_message(path, e.message)
end

def baseline_unreadable_message(path, why)
  "baseline #{relpath(path)} is present but unreadable (#{why}). Fix: restore it from git " \
    "(`git checkout origin/main -- #{relpath(path)}`) or re-capture it with `--run --update-baseline`; an " \
    "unreadable baseline is never treated as no baseline, because that would certify nothing and exit 0."
end

def load_baseline(path = baseline_path)
  doc = load_baseline_doc(path)
  doc && load_baseline_cases(doc)
end

def load_baseline_cases(doc)
  doc["cases"].each_with_object({}) { |c, h| h[c["name"]] = baseline_case(c) }
end

# One baseline entry. A rate that does not parse is kept as an ERROR on the
# case (diff_baseline refuses it), never dropped: a case the diff cannot score
# must not read as a case with nothing to compare.
def baseline_case(entry)
  BaselineCase.new(entry["pass"], EvalScore.parse_rate(entry["rate"]), nil, entry["fixture"])
rescue EvalScore::InvalidCounts => e
  BaselineCase.new(entry["pass"], nil, e.message, entry["fixture"])
end

# The baseline document --update-baseline writes (DND-522): the subject render,
# the FULL corpus fingerprint, the N it sampled, and each case's fixture
# fingerprint, so a later diff can tell a comparable case from one whose test
# changed under it.
def baseline_doc(results, subject:, corpus:, runs:, agent: eval_agent)
  {
    "generated" => Time.now.utc.iso8601,
    "agent" => agent.name,
    "subject" => subject,
    "corpus" => corpus,
    "runs" => runs,
    "cases" => results.map do |r|
      { "name" => r[:name], "pass" => r[:pass], "rate" => r[:rate], "fixture" => r[:fixture] }
    end
  }
end

# Why --update-baseline must not run with these options, or nil. A baseline
# describes the WHOLE corpus at the N the diff is calibrated at (DND-225/523):
# an --only capture overwrote every other case, and a small N made every later
# diff print the under-sampled Note.
def update_baseline_error(only:, runs:)
  if only
    return "--update-baseline with --only #{only.inspect} would rewrite the baseline with only the selected " \
           "case(s), dropping every other case's measurement. Fix: re-baseline the full corpus: drop --only."
  end
  return nil if runs >= DEFAULT_RUNS

  "--update-baseline at --runs #{runs} would record a baseline sampled below N=#{DEFAULT_RUNS}, the N the " \
    "diff's rule (EvalScore.classify) is calibrated at. Fix: re-baseline at the default --runs (#{DEFAULT_RUNS}) " \
    "or higher."
end

# How one baseline case compares with this run's result for it (nil = absent).
# Returns [flip, detail]; flip is an EvalScore.classify flip, :unusable,
# :changed (the case's fixture changed since the baseline, so its baseline rate
# scored a different test), or :same (a T1 case whose pass did not change).
def baseline_flip(base, result)
  return [:unusable, "baseline rate unusable: #{base.rate_error}"] if base.rate_error
  return [:missing, "missing from this run (baseline #{rate_text(base.counts)})"] if result.nil?
  if base.fixture && result[:fixture] && base.fixture != result[:fixture]
    return [:changed, "fixture changed since the baseline (#{base.fixture} -> #{result[:fixture]}); " \
                      "baseline #{rate_text(base.counts)} scored a different test"]
  end

  if result[:mode] == "hook-stdin"
    return [:regressed, "T1 baseline pass -> now fail"] if base.pass && !result[:pass]
    return [:improved, "T1 baseline fail -> now pass"] if !base.pass && result[:pass]

    return [:same, nil]
  end

  now = EvalScore.parse_rate(result[:rate])
  flip = EvalScore.classify(base.counts, now)
  return [flip, "never sampled this run (0/0; baseline #{rate_text(base.counts)})"] if flip == :missing
  return [flip, nil] if base.counts.nil? || now.nil?

  d, lo, hi = EvalScore.newcombe(base.counts, now)
  [flip, format("baseline %s -> now %s (delta %+.2f, 95%% [%+.2f, %+.2f])", rate_text(base.counts),
                rate_text(now), d, lo, hi)]
end

def rate_text(counts)
  counts ? "#{counts.k}/#{counts.n}" : "n/a"
end

# The comparison diff_baseline prints and compare_to_baseline certifies, as
# data. Pure: no output. `unmeasured` is every case this run scored that no
# comparable baseline measurement covers (new to the corpus, or its fixture
# changed); `compared` counts the cases both sides measured on the same fixture.
def baseline_comparison(results, baseline, only: nil)
  now = results.to_h { |r| [r[:name], r] }
  # --only compares only what it selected; every other baseline case is out of
  # scope, not missing.
  scoped = baseline.select { |name, _| only.nil? || name.start_with?(only) }
  flips = scoped.to_h { |name, base| [name, baseline_flip(base, now[name])] }
  with_flip = ->(*kinds) { flips.select { |_, (f, _)| kinds.include?(f) } }
  new_cases = (now.keys - baseline.keys) + with_flip.call(:new).keys
  changed = with_flip.call(:changed)
  unmeasured = new_cases + changed.keys
  {
    now: now, scoped: scoped,
    unusable: with_flip.call(:unusable),
    regressed: flips.select { |_, (f, _)| EvalScore.regression?(f) },
    improved: with_flip.call(:improved),
    new_cases: new_cases, changed: changed,
    unmeasured_failing: unmeasured.reject { |n| now[n] && now[n][:pass] },
    compared: flips.count { |_, (f, _)| !%i[unusable missing changed new].include?(f) }
  }
end

def diff_baseline(results, baseline, agent: eval_agent, only: nil)
  cmp = baseline_comparison(results, baseline, only: only)
  now = cmp[:now]
  unusable = cmp[:unusable]
  regressed = cmp[:regressed]
  puts ""
  puts "baseline diff: #{regressed.size} regressed, #{cmp[:improved].size} improved, " \
       "#{cmp[:new_cases].size} new case(s), #{cmp[:changed].size} changed fixture(s) " \
       "(T2: EvalScore.classify on counts; T1: exact)"
  cmp[:improved].each { |n, (_, why)| puts "  + improved: #{n} #{why}" }
  cmp[:new_cases].each { |n| puts "  · new:   #{n} (#{now[n] && now[n][:pass] ? 'pass' : 'fail'}; no baseline measurement)" }
  cmp[:changed].each { |n, (_, why)| puts "  · changed: #{n} (#{now[n][:pass] ? 'pass' : 'fail'}; #{why})" }
  report_undersampled(cmp[:scoped].reject { |n, _| cmp[:changed].key?(n) }, now)

  unless unusable.empty?
    warn "admiral-eval: FAILED — #{unusable.size} baseline case(s) cannot be scored:"
    unusable.each { |n, (_, why)| warn "  - #{n}: #{why}" }
    warn "  Fix: every baseline case needs a \"rate\" of \"<passes>/<samples>\". Re-capture the baseline " \
         "against the current #{agent.name} with `#{rerun_hint(agent)} --run --update-baseline` once this run is " \
         "green; do not hand-edit a rate to make the diff pass."
  end
  unless regressed.empty?
    warn "admiral-eval: FAILED — regression vs baseline:"
    regressed.each { |n, (_, why)| warn "  - #{n}: #{why}" }
    warn "  Fix: a change (likely a #{agent.name} trim or block edit) broke a behavioral case the baseline caught. The rule " \
         "already discounts sampling noise (EvalScore.classify: the drop is outside the 95% band or exceeds " \
         "0.3), so restore the invariant's TRIGGER (the resident one-liner / JIT-skill pointer that fires it), " \
         "not just its words; do NOT run --update-baseline to hide it. A missing case means its fixture or " \
         "its samples disappeared: restore them. If either side was sampled below #{DEFAULT_RUNS} (see the " \
         "Note above), re-run `--run --only <case>` at the default --runs to confirm."
  end
  report_unmeasured_failing(cmp[:unmeasured_failing], now, agent)
  unusable.empty? && regressed.empty? && cmp[:unmeasured_failing].empty? ? 0 : 1
end

# DND-522: a case no comparable baseline measurement covers is judged like a
# run with no baseline at all: its majority must pass. Before, a failing new
# case printed "0 regressed" and exited 0.
def report_unmeasured_failing(names, now, agent)
  return if names.empty?

  warn "admiral-eval: FAILED — #{names.size} case(s) FAIL that no baseline measurement covers:"
  names.each { |n| warn "  - #{n}: #{now[n] ? "#{now[n][:rate]} #{now[n][:detail]}" : 'not run'}" }
  warn "  Fix: nothing certifies these cases, so the failure is not noise against a baseline. Make the " \
       "#{agent.name} pass them (restore the invariant's trigger), or fix the fixture if it is wrong; then " \
       "re-capture with `#{rerun_hint(agent)} --run --update-baseline` so the baseline measures them."
end

# The ONE certification line for a diff (DND-522). "0 regressed" alone read the
# same whether the baseline measured this render, this corpus and every case,
# or none of them. So every diff ends in exactly one `baseline verdict:` line:
#   FAILED       — the diff exits 1 (regression, missing, unscorable, or an
#                  uncovered failing case);
#   CERTIFIED    — same subject sha, same corpus fingerprint, every case
#                  compared on its baseline fixture, no --only;
#   UNCERTIFIED  — exit 0, but at least one gap, each named, with a Fix:.
# A subject or corpus mismatch is a LOUD FLAG, not a refusal: a before/after
# check of a deliberate agent change runs a different render by design (and
# variant-eval runs a variant render against this baseline), so refusing it
# would make that exit code meaningless. The flag says what "0 regressed" then
# covers, and what it does not.
def compare_to_baseline(results, doc, subject:, corpus:, only: nil, agent: eval_agent)
  cases = load_baseline_cases(doc)
  code = diff_baseline(results, cases, agent: agent, only: only)
  subject_state = report_provenance(doc["subject"], subject, agent: agent)
  corpus_state = report_corpus(doc["corpus"], corpus)
  cmp = baseline_comparison(results, cases, only: only)
  report_verdict(code, cmp, subject_state: subject_state, corpus_state: corpus_state, only: only, agent: agent)
  code
end

def report_corpus(recorded, current)
  if recorded.nil?
    puts "baseline corpus: UNKNOWN — this baseline predates corpus fingerprinting."
    return :unknown
  end
  if recorded == current
    puts "baseline corpus: #{current} (matches this corpus)"
    return :match
  end
  puts "baseline corpus: CHANGED — captured against corpus #{recorded}, this corpus is #{current}."
  :changed
end

def certification_gaps(cmp, subject_state:, corpus_state:, only:)
  names = ->(list) { list.first(5).join(", ") + (list.size > 5 ? ", ..." : "") }
  gaps = []
  gaps << "subject #{subject_state.to_s.upcase}" unless subject_state == :match
  gaps << "corpus #{corpus_state.to_s.upcase}" unless corpus_state == :match
  unless cmp[:new_cases].empty?
    gaps << "#{cmp[:new_cases].size} case(s) the baseline never measured (#{names.(cmp[:new_cases])})"
  end
  unless cmp[:changed].empty?
    gaps << "#{cmp[:changed].size} case(s) whose fixture changed since the baseline (#{names.(cmp[:changed].keys)})"
  end
  gaps << "--only #{only}: a partial run, not the corpus" if only
  gaps
end

def report_verdict(code, cmp, subject_state:, corpus_state:, only:, agent:)
  gaps = certification_gaps(cmp, subject_state: subject_state, corpus_state: corpus_state, only: only)
  if code != 0
    puts "baseline verdict: FAILED — see the FAILED list above#{gaps.empty? ? '' : "; also: #{gaps.join('; ')}"}."
  elsif gaps.empty?
    puts "baseline verdict: CERTIFIED — #{cmp[:compared]} case(s) compared against the baseline's own subject " \
         "and corpus; 0 regressed."
  else
    puts "baseline verdict: UNCERTIFIED — #{gaps.join('; ')}. '0 regressed' covers only the #{cmp[:compared]} " \
         "case(s) both sides measured on the same fixture, against the RECORDED subject; it certifies nothing else."
    warn "admiral-eval: Note: the baseline diff is UNCERTIFIED (#{gaps.join('; ')}). " \
         "Fix: once a full run is green, re-capture with `#{rerun_hint(agent)} --run --update-baseline` (full " \
         "corpus, --runs >= #{DEFAULT_RUNS}) at the render that lands. If you changed the #{agent.name} on purpose, " \
         "a STALE subject is the before/after shape: confirm the baseline's sha is the render on origin/main " \
         "before reading '0 regressed' as no regression."
  end
end

# Advisory (never an exit-code change): EvalScore.classify is calibrated at
# N = DEFAULT_RUNS per side. A smaller side (a legacy K=3 baseline, or --runs 3)
# is still classified, but its interval is wider and so is its false-flag rate.
# Say so rather than let the diff read as calibrated.
def report_undersampled(scoped, now)
  small = scoped.select do |name, base|
    r = now[name]
    next false if base.counts.nil? || r.nil? || r[:mode] == "hook-stdin"

    var = EvalScore.parse_rate(r[:rate])
    base.counts.n < DEFAULT_RUNS || (var && var.n < DEFAULT_RUNS)
  end
  return if small.empty?

  ex_name, ex_base = small.first
  warn "admiral-eval: Note: #{small.size} T2 case(s) compared with a side sampled below N=#{DEFAULT_RUNS} " \
       "(e.g. #{ex_name}: baseline #{rate_text(ex_base.counts)}, now #{now[ex_name][:rate]}). EvalScore.classify " \
       "is calibrated at N=#{DEFAULT_RUNS} per side (DND-225); a smaller side widens the false-flag rate. " \
       "Fix: run at the default --runs, and re-capture the baseline at N=#{DEFAULT_RUNS} with " \
       "`--run --update-baseline` once a run is green."
end

# --- baseline PROVENANCE ----------------------------------------------------
# A baseline records which cases passed, but until DND/2026-09-20 it recorded
# nothing about WHICH admiral produced them. That made two very different
# states byte-indistinguishable: a baseline captured against TODAY's agent, and
# one captured against an agent that has since been rewritten. Both print
# "0 regressed", and only the first one means anything.
#
# Measured instance: PR #32 captured an all-green baseline against the 1034-line
# admiral; PR #33 then trimmed the rendered agent to 496 lines (a 52% cut of
# resident prose) and asserted the trim was "behavior-preserving". Nothing
# re-measured it, and nothing could have NOTICED that nothing had — which is
# this repo's "a failed lookup must never look like an empty one" class, and the
# converse of harness-IA #8 (measure the win, don't assert it).
#
# So the baseline now fingerprints its subject, and a run says which of the
# three states it is in. A mismatch never changes the exit code by itself (a
# before/after check of a deliberate agent change runs a different render by
# design); since DND-522 it makes compare_to_baseline's one `baseline verdict:`
# line read UNCERTIFIED, so a stale baseline can no longer read as a clean one.
def subject_fingerprint(path = default_agent_file)
  return { "file" => relpath(path), "status" => "missing" } unless File.file?(path)
  body = File.read(path)
  {
    "file"   => relpath(path),
    "lines"  => body.lines.size,
    "sha256" => Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(body)
  }
end

def relpath(path)
  root = File.expand_path("..", AI_DIR)
  path.start_with?(root + "/") ? path[(root.size + 1)..] : path
end

# Prints the provenance verdict. Returns the verdict symbol so the self-test can
# assert all three WITHOUT depending on the exact wording.
def report_provenance(recorded, current = subject_fingerprint, agent: eval_agent)
  if recorded.nil?
    puts "baseline subject: UNKNOWN — this baseline predates subject fingerprinting."
    warn "admiral-eval: Note: the baseline does not record which #{agent.name} it was captured against, " \
         "so a clean diff does NOT certify today's agent. " \
         "Fix: re-capture it against the current agent with `#{rerun_hint(agent)} --run --update-baseline` " \
         "once this run is green; after that, drift is reported automatically."
    return :unknown
  end

  if recorded["sha256"] == current["sha256"]
    puts "baseline subject: #{current['file']} #{current['lines']} lines " \
         "sha #{current['sha256'][0, 12]} (matches this run)"
    return :match
  end

  puts "baseline subject: STALE — captured against a different #{agent.name}."
  warn "admiral-eval: Note: the baseline was captured against #{recorded['file']} at " \
       "#{recorded['lines'] || '?'} lines (sha #{(recorded['sha256'] || '?')[0, 12]}), but this run scored " \
       "#{current['lines'] || '?'} lines (sha #{(current['sha256'] || '?')[0, 12]}). The agent changed since the " \
       "baseline, so '0 regressed' above compares today's results against a DIFFERENT agent's expectations — " \
       "it is not evidence that the change preserved behavior. " \
       "Fix: if this run is green, re-capture with `#{rerun_hint(agent)} --run --update-baseline` to certify the " \
       "current agent; if it is NOT green, treat the failures as caused by the agent change and restore the " \
       "invariant's trigger rather than re-baselining over it."
  :stale
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Numeric score (DND-225). Each sampled (T2) case scores its pass-rate k/n with
# a Wilson 95% interval; the aggregate is the mean per-case pass-rate over the
# SAMPLED cases. T1 cases are deterministic (zero run-to-run variance, measured)
# and reported apart. A case that sampled nothing (0/0) is n/a, never 0. The
# aggregate is comparable across runs ONLY at the same corpus fingerprint and
# the same admiral sha. Method, measured band, and the delta rule:
# ai/docs/eval-noise-band.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def score_lines(results, corpus_id)
  sampled = results.reject { |r| r[:mode] == "hook-stdin" }
  t1 = results.select { |r| r[:mode] == "hook-stdin" }
  counts = sampled.map { |r| EvalScore.parse_rate(r[:rate]) }
  agg = EvalScore.aggregate(counts)
  head = agg[:score] ? format("%.2f", agg[:score]) : "n/a"
  lines = ["score (pass-rate, Wilson 95%): aggregate #{head} over #{agg[:measured]} sampled case(s), " \
           "n/a: #{agg[:n_a]}; T1 deterministic: #{t1.count { |r| r[:pass] }}/#{t1.size} pass; corpus #{corpus_id}"]
  sampled.zip(counts).each do |r, c|
    lines << format("  %-34s %s", r[:name], EvalScore.format_score(EvalScore.case_score(c)))
  end
  lines
end

# One case's fixture fingerprint (DND-522): the same digest over just its dir.
# The baseline records it per case, so a diff can tell a case whose test
# changed under it from one it can compare.
def fixture_fingerprint(dir)
  corpus_fingerprint([dir])
end

# sha256 over every fixture file under the selected fixture dirs (path + bytes),
# first 12 hex. Two aggregates are comparable only when this matches.
def corpus_fingerprint(dirs)
  digest = Digest::SHA256.new
  dirs.sort.each do |d|
    Dir.glob(File.join(d, "**", "*")).sort.select { |f| File.file?(f) }.each do |f|
      digest << relpath(f) << "\0" << File.binread(f) << "\0"
    end
  end
  digest.hexdigest[0, 12]
end

def run_model(runs:, only:, update:, agent: eval_agent, agent_file: default_agent_file(agent))
  dirs = fixtures(agent)
  dirs = dirs.select { |d| File.basename(d).start_with?(only) } if only
  if dirs.empty?
    warn "admiral-eval: no fixtures#{only ? " matching #{only.inspect}" : ''} under #{agent.fixtures_rel} " \
         "(agent #{agent.key}) — zero cases is not a run."
    warn "  Fix: add #{agent.fixtures_rel}/<NAME>/meta (+ scenario.md or input.json), or fix --only."
    return 1
  end

  # Build the subject argv ONCE, before any model call: a subject that cannot
  # load fails here, loudly, instead of per case.
  argv = model_argv(agent_file, agent)
  subject = subject_fingerprint(agent_file)
  # DND-522: read the baseline BEFORE any model call. A present-but-unreadable
  # baseline is an error, never "no baseline committed yet".
  begin
    baseline = update ? nil : load_baseline_doc(baseline_path(agent))
  rescue BaselineUnreadable => e
    warn "admiral-eval: FAILED — #{e.message}"
    return 1
  end
  # variant-eval parses this line (confirm_t2_subject!) to prove which render ran;
  # keep its `subject: ... sha <12 hex> (loaded inline via --agents` shape.
  puts "agent: #{agent.key} (#{agent.name}), corpus #{agent.fixtures_rel}"
  puts "subject: #{subject['file']} #{subject['lines']} lines sha #{subject['sha256'][0, 12]} " \
       "(loaded inline via --agents as #{agent.subject_key})"
  # DND-525: a ~60min --run prints nothing until every case finishes, so a hung
  # run looks the same as a slow one from outside. Print a per-case completion
  # line as each case finishes, to STDERR only — variant-eval's collect_t2_counts
  # (ai/bin/variant-eval) parses admiral-eval's STDOUT (the subject line, the
  # PASS/FAIL rows, and the closing `admiral-eval: P/T cases pass` summary) and
  # must see it byte-identical; these lines never touch STDOUT.
  total = dirs.size
  usage_log = [] # DND-524: one entry per T2 model call this run makes (nil = no usage reported)
  results = dirs.each_with_index.map do |d, i|
    r = evaluate(d, runs: runs, argv: argv, agent: agent, usage_log: usage_log).merge(fixture: fixture_fingerprint(d))
    warn "  [#{i + 1}/#{total}] #{r[:name]}: #{r[:pass] ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'} (#{r[:rate]})"
    r
  end
  warn usage_summary_line(usage_log)
  results.each do |r|
    printf("%-4s %-34s %-6s [%s] %s\n", r[:pass] ? "PASS" : "FAIL", r[:name], r[:rate], r[:mode], r[:detail])
  end
  puts "admiral-eval: #{results.count { |r| r[:pass] }}/#{results.size} cases pass (runs/T2 case = #{runs})"
  score_lines(results, corpus_fingerprint(dirs)).each { |l| puts l }

  if update
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(baseline_path(agent)))
    File.write(baseline_path(agent), JSON.pretty_generate(
      baseline_doc(results, subject: subject, corpus: corpus_fingerprint(fixtures(agent)), runs: runs, agent: agent)
    ) + "\n")
    puts "admiral-eval: baseline written to #{agent.baseline_rel} " \
         "(subject: #{subject['file']} #{subject['lines']} lines sha #{(subject['sha256'] || '?')[0, 12]})"
    return 0
  end

  if baseline
    return compare_to_baseline(results, baseline, subject: subject, corpus: corpus_fingerprint(fixtures(agent)),
                                                  only: only, agent: agent)
  end

  failures = results.reject { |r| r[:pass] }
  return 0 if failures.empty?
  warn ""
  warn "admiral-eval: #{failures.size} case(s) failed (no baseline committed yet — reporting only):"
  failures.each { |r| warn "  - #{r[:name]}: #{r[:detail]}" }
  warn "  Fix: this run has no #{agent.baseline_rel} to diff against. Capture the baseline " \
       "against the CURRENT #{agent.name} with `#{rerun_hint(agent)} --run --update-baseline` once the cases " \
       "pass on today's #{agent.name}; never baseline a case that cannot pass reliably (mark it advisory)."
  1
end

def list(agent = eval_agent)
  fx = fixtures(agent)
  if fx.empty?
    warn "admiral-eval: no fixtures under #{agent.fixtures_rel} for #{agent.name}."
    warn "  Fix: add #{agent.fixtures_rel}/<NAME>/meta (+ scenario.md); an agent with zero cases has no " \
         "behavioral eval, and listing it as empty would read as a covered agent."
    return 1
  end
  puts "admiral-eval: #{fx.size} fixture(s) for #{agent.name} (--agent #{agent.key}; also: " \
       "#{(EvalSubject.keys_for_runner(RUNNER) - [agent.key]).join(', ')}):"
  fx.each do |d|
    m = parse_meta(File.join(d, "meta"))
    puts "  - #{File.basename(d)} [#{m['mode']}] #{m['invariant']}"
  end
  puts ""
  puts "Trust model: the model PRODUCES a decision; a DETERMINISTIC scorer scores it."
  puts "  Subject: the rendered #{agent.name} named by --agent-file (default this checkout's"
  puts "  #{agent.render_rel}), passed inline via --agents, never resolved from"
  puts "  ~/.claude/agents (the main checkout's copy)."
  puts "  The model runs under the AMBIENT HOME (required for claude auth; design"
  puts "  §2.1's isolated HOME is infeasible). Tool containment is"
  puts "  STRUCTURAL, via CLI flags that hold regardless of the ambient settings:"
  puts "    --strict-mcp-config --mcp-config {empty}  no MCP server loads (no Notion/Slack/Agent)"
  puts "    --disallowedTools Bash Task               no shell (no absolute-path bypass), no subagent spawn"
  puts "  So a run cannot reach MCP, spawn a captain, or run Bash. Guard 1 (plan-only"
  puts "  framing) is the primary framing; the inert PATH stubs (no-op gh/glab/docker/"
  puts "  git-push) are a residual layer. --run is model-in-loop and NOT in the gate;"
  puts "  the deterministic --self-test IS gate-wired."
  puts "Run the model-in-loop evaluation with: #{rerun_hint(agent)} --run"
  0
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --self-test — proves the scorer / parser / sampling / baseline diff / T1
# primitive / inert sandbox, with NO model in the loop.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def self_test
  ok = true
  adm = eval_agent(DEFAULT_AGENT)
  fail = ->(msg) { warn "self-test: FAIL — #{msg}"; ok = false }

  # -- 1. trailer parser ---------------------------------------------------
  p = parse_trailer("intro prose\nNEXT_ACTIONS: confirm-merged, notion.set-status:Done\ntrailing")
  fail.("actions parse wrong: #{p[:actions].inspect}") \
    unless p[:actions] == ["confirm-merged", "notion.set-status:Done"]
  fail.("labeled answer parse wrong") \
    unless parse_trailer("MODEL_CHOICE: opus\n")[:answers]["MODEL_CHOICE"] == "opus"
  fail.("`NEXT_ACTIONS: none` must be empty") unless parse_trailer("NEXT_ACTIONS: none")[:actions].empty?
  fail.("absent trailer must be empty") unless parse_trailer("no trailer at all")[:actions].empty?

  # -- 2. action matcher ---------------------------------------------------
  fail.("bare spec must match any arg") unless action_matches?("notion.set-status", "notion.set-status:Done")
  fail.("arg spec must match exact arg") unless action_matches?("notion.set-status:Done", "notion.set-status:Done")
  fail.("arg spec must reject other arg") if action_matches?("notion.set-status:Done", "notion.set-status:InReview")
  fail.("head mismatch must not match") if action_matches?("merge-mr", "confirm-merged")

  # -- 3. scorer primitives ------------------------------------------------
  # expect_action hit / miss
  hit = score_decision({ "mode" => "next-action", "expect_action" => ["confirm-merged"] },
                       { actions: ["confirm-merged"], answers: {} })
  fail.("expect_action hit should pass") unless hit[0]
  miss = score_decision({ "mode" => "next-action", "expect_action" => ["confirm-merged"] },
                        { actions: ["wait"], answers: {} })
  fail.("expect_action miss should fail") if miss[0]

  # forbid_action absent (pass) / present (fail)
  fb_ok = score_decision({ "mode" => "next-action", "expect_action" => ["wait"], "forbid_action" => ["merge-mr"] },
                         { actions: ["wait"], answers: {} })
  fail.("forbid_action absent should pass") unless fb_ok[0]
  fb_bad = score_decision({ "mode" => "next-action", "expect_action" => ["wait"], "forbid_action" => ["merge-mr"] },
                          { actions: ["wait", "merge-mr"], answers: {} })
  fail.("forbid_action present should fail") if fb_bad[0]

  # expect_order satisfied / violated / missing
  ord_ok = score_decision({ "mode" => "next-action", "expect_order" => ["confirm-merged < notion.set-status:Done"] },
                          { actions: ["confirm-merged", "notion.set-status:Done"], answers: {} })
  fail.("order satisfied should pass") unless ord_ok[0]
  ord_bad = score_decision({ "mode" => "next-action", "expect_order" => ["confirm-merged < notion.set-status:Done"] },
                           { actions: ["notion.set-status:Done", "confirm-merged"], answers: {} })
  fail.("order violated should fail") if ord_bad[0]
  ord_missing = score_decision({ "mode" => "next-action", "expect_order" => ["confirm-merged < notion.set-status:Done"] },
                               { actions: ["confirm-merged"], answers: {} })
  fail.("order with a missing side should fail") if ord_missing[0]

  # answer_field match / mismatch
  ans_ok = score_decision({ "mode" => "structured-answer", "answer_field" => "MODEL_CHOICE", "expect_answer" => "opus" },
                          { actions: [], answers: { "MODEL_CHOICE" => "Opus" } })
  fail.("answer match (case-insensitive) should pass") unless ans_ok[0]
  ans_bad = score_decision({ "mode" => "structured-answer", "answer_field" => "MODEL_CHOICE", "expect_answer" => "opus" },
                           { actions: [], answers: { "MODEL_CHOICE" => "sonnet" } })
  fail.("answer mismatch should fail") if ans_bad[0]

  # -- 4. missing decision is a fail (not a pass) --------------------------
  # a forbid-only next-action case with an EMPTY trailer must fail, not vacuously pass.
  empty_na = score_decision({ "mode" => "next-action", "forbid_action" => ["slack.dm-owner"] },
                            { actions: [], answers: {} })
  fail.("empty NEXT_ACTIONS must fail even for a forbid-only case") if empty_na[0]
  empty_sa = score_decision({ "mode" => "structured-answer", "answer_field" => "MODEL_CHOICE", "expect_answer" => "opus" },
                            { actions: [], answers: {} })
  fail.("missing answer line must fail") if empty_sa[0]

  # -- 5. majority sampling ------------------------------------------------
  fail.("{pass,pass,fail} must be a majority pass") unless majority([true, true, false])
  fail.("{pass,fail,fail} must be a majority fail") if majority([true, false, false])

  # -- 6. meta parser: repeatable keys accumulate -------------------------
  Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-meta-") do |d|
    mp = File.join(d, "meta")
    File.write(mp, "mode=next-action\nexpect_action=confirm-merged\nexpect_action=sweep-fleet\nforbid_action=merge-mr\n")
    m = parse_meta(mp)
    fail.("repeatable expect_action should be a 2-list: #{m['expect_action'].inspect}") \
      unless m["expect_action"] == %w[confirm-merged sweep-fleet]
    fail.("scalar mode should be a string") unless m["mode"] == "next-action"
  end

  # -- 7. baseline diff ----------------------------------------------------
  one = EvalScore.counts(1, 1)
  base = { "AE-01" => BaselineCase.new(true, one), "AE-02" => BaselineCase.new(true, one) }
  regressed_run = [{ name: "AE-01", mode: "hook-stdin", pass: true, rate: "1/1" },
                   { name: "AE-02", mode: "hook-stdin", pass: false, rate: "1/1" }]
  code, out, err = capture_io { diff_baseline(regressed_run, base) }
  fail.("a baseline pass->fail must exit 1") unless code == 1
  fail.("regression must name the regressed case") unless err.include?("AE-02")
  fail.("regression Fix: line missing") unless err.include?("Fix:")
  # a case the baseline FAILED that now passes is an improvement (reported, exit 0)
  base_fixed = { "AE-01" => BaselineCase.new(true, one), "AE-02" => BaselineCase.new(false, one) }
  fixed_run = [{ name: "AE-01", mode: "hook-stdin", pass: true, rate: "1/1" },
               { name: "AE-02", mode: "hook-stdin", pass: true, rate: "1/1" }]
  code2, out2, = capture_io { diff_baseline(fixed_run, base_fixed) }
  fail.("a newly-fixed case must exit 0") unless code2 == 0
  fail.("fixed case must be reported") unless out2.include?("+ improved: AE-02")

  # -- 7c. baseline diff is EvalScore.classify on counts (DND-523) ----------
  # The defect: the diff compared K-sample MAJORITY booleans, so a stochastic
  # case (AE-18, p≈0.78) baselined as a pass read "pass -> fail" on ~13% of
  # K=3 runs with nothing changed. The fix scores each T2 case with
  # EvalScore.classify (Newcombe 95% interval below 0 OR a drop > 0.3; a missing
  # case is a regression) at N = variant-eval's DEFAULT_RUNS. The same fixtures
  # prove the rule is not weakened: a real drop is still flagged, including one
  # the majority rule MISSED. Counts only — no model.
  Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-classify-") do |d|
    load_base = lambda do |cases|
      path = File.join(d, "baseline-#{cases.hash.abs}.json")
      File.write(path, JSON.generate("cases" => cases.map { |n, pass, rate| { "name" => n, "pass" => pass, "rate" => rate } }))
      load_baseline(path)
    end
    row = ->(n, pass, rate, mode = "next-action") { { name: n, mode: mode, pass: pass, rate: rate } }
    diff = lambda do |results, base, **kw|
      capture_io { diff_baseline(results, base, **kw) }
    rescue StandardError => e
      [:raised, "", "#{e.class}: #{e.message}"]
    end

    # (a) AE-18 shape, N=10 baseline: baselined pass 8/10, now 5/10 (a majority
    #     FAIL). Inside the noise band (Δ=-0.3, interval spans 0) -> no flag.
    c, _o, e = diff.([row.("AE-18", false, "5/10")], load_base.([["AE-18", true, "8/10"]]))
    fail.("AE-18-shape 8/10 -> 5/10 is sampling noise and must NOT flag (exit #{c.inspect}): #{e}") unless c == 0
    c, _o, e = diff.([row.("AE-18", true, "7/10")], load_base.([["AE-18", true, "10/10"]]))
    fail.("AE-18-shape 10/10 -> 7/10 must NOT flag (exit #{c.inspect}): #{e}") unless c == 0

    # (b) legacy K=3 baseline (today's admiral-baseline.json format): the AE-02
    #     2/3 shape against a default run of 4/10 is noise -> no flag.
    c, _o, e = diff.([row.("AE-02", false, "4/10")], load_base.([["AE-02", true, "2/3"]]))
    fail.("legacy 2/3 -> 4/10 is inside the band and must NOT flag (exit #{c.inspect}): #{e}") unless c == 0

    # (c) a REAL drop is still caught: 10/10 -> 5/10, and 10/10 -> 6/10, which
    #     the majority rule missed (6/10 is a majority pass).
    c, _o, e = diff.([row.("AE-01", false, "5/10")], load_base.([["AE-01", true, "10/10"]]))
    fail.("a real drop 10/10 -> 5/10 must exit 1 (got #{c.inspect})") unless c == 1
    fail.("a flagged drop must name the case and both counts: #{e}") \
      unless e.include?("AE-01") && e.include?("10/10") && e.include?("5/10")
    fail.("a flagged drop must carry a Fix: line") unless e.include?("Fix:")
    c, _o, = diff.([row.("AE-01", true, "6/10")], load_base.([["AE-01", true, "10/10"]]))
    fail.("a real drop 10/10 -> 6/10 (a majority pass) must still exit 1 (got #{c.inspect})") unless c == 1
    c, _o, = diff.([row.("AE-01", true, "6/10")], load_base.([["AE-01", true, "3/3"]]))
    fail.("legacy 3/3 -> 6/10 is a drop of 0.4 and must exit 1 (got #{c.inspect})") unless c == 1

    # (d) a MISSING case is a regression: never sampled (0/0), or absent from
    #     a full run although the baseline has it.
    c, _o, e = diff.([row.("AE-01", false, "0/0")], load_base.([["AE-01", true, "10/10"]]))
    fail.("an unsampled (0/0) case must exit 1 as missing (got #{c.inspect}): #{e}") unless c == 1
    c, _o, e = diff.([row.("AE-01", true, "10/10")],
                     load_base.([["AE-01", true, "10/10"], ["AE-02", true, "10/10"]]))
    fail.("a baseline case absent from a full run must exit 1 as missing (got #{c.inspect})") unless c == 1
    fail.("a missing case must be named: #{e}") unless e.include?("AE-02")
    # ...but --only scopes the comparison: cases it did not select are not missing.
    c, _o, e = diff.([row.("AE-01", true, "10/10")],
                     load_base.([["AE-01", true, "10/10"], ["AE-02", true, "10/10"]]), only: "AE-01")
    fail.("--only must not report unselected baseline cases as missing (got #{c.inspect}): #{e}") unless c == 0

    # (e) T1 (hook-stdin) is deterministic and its rate is always 1/1, so it
    #     must diff EXACTLY on pass, never through classify (1/1 vs 1/1).
    c, _o, = diff.([row.("AE-13a", false, "1/1", "hook-stdin")], load_base.([["AE-13a", true, "1/1"]]))
    fail.("a T1 baseline pass -> fail must exit 1 even though both rates read 1/1 (got #{c.inspect})") \
      unless c == 1

    # (f) an improvement is reported only when classify says so; a new case is n/a.
    c, o, = diff.([row.("AE-19c", true, "10/10"), row.("AE-99", true, "10/10")],
                  load_base.([["AE-19c", false, "0/10"]]))
    fail.("0/10 -> 10/10 must be reported improved, exit 0 (got #{c.inspect}): #{o}") \
      unless c == 0 && o.include?("+ improved: AE-19c")
    fail.("a case with no baseline must be reported new: #{o}") unless o.include?("new:   AE-99")

    # (g) a baseline case with no usable rate cannot be classified: refuse loudly,
    #     never fall back to "no baseline" (a failed lookup must not read empty).
    c, _o, e = diff.([row.("AE-01", true, "10/10")], load_base.([["AE-01", true, nil]]))
    fail.("a rate-less baseline case must exit 1 with a Fix:, not pass (got #{c.inspect}): #{e}") \
      unless c == 1 && e.include?("Fix:")
  end

  # The rule is calibrated at variant-eval's N (DND-225); the two defaults must
  # not drift apart silently.
  ve = File.read(File.join(AI_DIR, "bin", "variant-eval"))[/^DEFAULT_RUNS\s*=\s*(\d+)/, 1]
  fail.("could not read variant-eval's DEFAULT_RUNS — the calibration cross-check cannot run") if ve.nil?
  fail.("admiral-eval DEFAULT_RUNS (#{DEFAULT_RUNS}) must equal variant-eval's (#{ve}), the N EvalScore.classify " \
        "is calibrated at (DND-225)") unless ve.nil? || DEFAULT_RUNS == ve.to_i

  # -- 7b. baseline PROVENANCE: a stale baseline must not read as a clean one --
  # The defect this guards: a baseline captured against a DIFFERENT admiral
  # still prints "0 regressed", so a 52%-trim (PR #32 baseline vs PR #33 agent)
  # looked identical to a verified one. Assert the MISS, not just the hit.
  Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-prov-") do |d|
    a = File.join(d, "agent-a.md")
    b = File.join(d, "agent-b.md")
    File.write(a, "line1\nline2\nline3\n")
    File.write(b, "line1\n")

    fp_a = subject_fingerprint(a)
    fp_b = subject_fingerprint(b)
    fail.("fingerprint must record line count") unless fp_a["lines"] == 3
    fail.("fingerprint must record a sha256") unless fp_a["sha256"] =~ /\A[0-9a-f]{64}\z/
    fail.("different agents must fingerprint differently") if fp_a["sha256"] == fp_b["sha256"]
    fail.("an absent subject must be status=missing, never a silent match") \
      unless subject_fingerprint(File.join(d, "nope.md"))["status"] == "missing"

    # match: same agent -> :match, and NOTHING on stderr
    vcode, vout, verr = capture_io { report_provenance(fp_a, fp_a) }
    fail.("same subject must verdict :match (got #{vcode.inspect})") unless vcode == :match
    fail.("a matching provenance must not warn: #{verr.inspect}") unless verr.strip.empty?
    fail.("a matching provenance must name the subject") unless vout.include?("matches this run")

    # stale: baseline captured against a DIFFERENT agent -> :stale + Fix:
    scode, sout, serr = capture_io { report_provenance(fp_a, fp_b) }
    fail.("a changed subject must verdict :stale (got #{scode.inspect})") unless scode == :stale
    fail.("a stale baseline must say so on stdout") unless sout.include?("STALE")
    fail.("a stale baseline must carry a Fix: line") unless serr.include?("Fix:")
    fail.("a stale baseline must report BOTH line counts") \
      unless serr.include?("3 lines") && serr.include?("1 lines")

    # unknown: a pre-fingerprinting baseline must SAY unknown, not read as match
    ucode, uout, uerr = capture_io { report_provenance(nil, fp_a) }
    fail.("a baseline with no subject must verdict :unknown (got #{ucode.inspect})") unless ucode == :unknown
    fail.("an unknown provenance must say UNKNOWN") unless uout.include?("UNKNOWN")
    fail.("an unknown provenance must carry a Fix: line") unless uerr.include?("Fix:")
  end

  # -- 7d. baseline CERTIFICATION: every coverage gap reads distinct from a
  #        clean "0 regressed" (DND-522) -------------------------------------
  # The defect: admiral-baseline.json was captured against render 76505fe5 over
  # 19 cases; the corpus grew (AE-18..AE-20) and the render moved on, yet a diff
  # printed "0 regressed" — for cases the baseline never measured, and a
  # failing unmeasured case still exited 0. The four states the ticket names
  # (subject mismatch, corpus mismatch, a case the baseline lacks, a case the
  # run lacks) must each produce a verdict line that is NOT "CERTIFIED", with a
  # Fix:; a failing case nothing certifies must fail; only the fully-covered,
  # same-subject, same-corpus state certifies. Counts only — no model.
  Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-certify-") do |d|
    fx = lambda do |name, body|
      dir = File.join(d, "fixtures", name)
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(dir)
      File.write(File.join(dir, "meta"), body)
      dir
    end
    f1 = fx.("AE-01", "mode: next-action\n")
    f2 = fx.("AE-02", "mode: next-action\n")
    f3 = fx.("AE-03", "mode: next-action\n")
    fp1 = fixture_fingerprint(f1)
    fp1b = fixture_fingerprint(fx.("AE-01b", "mode: next-action\nchanged: yes\n"))
    fail.("fixture_fingerprint must differ for different fixture bytes") if fp1 == fp1b
    subj = { "file" => "ai/agents/athena-admiral.md", "lines" => 3, "sha256" => "a" * 64 }
    other = { "file" => "ai/agents/athena-admiral.md", "lines" => 9, "sha256" => "b" * 64 }
    row = ->(n, pass, rate, fp) { { name: n, mode: "next-action", pass: pass, rate: rate, fixture: fp } }
    run2 = [row.("AE-01", true, "10/10", fp1), row.("AE-02", true, "10/10", fixture_fingerprint(f2))]
    doc = baseline_doc(run2, subject: subj, corpus: "c0rpus", runs: 10, agent: adm)
    fail.("baseline_doc must record the corpus fingerprint: #{doc.inspect}") unless doc["corpus"] == "c0rpus"
    fail.("baseline_doc must record each case's fixture fingerprint: #{doc['cases'].inspect}") \
      unless doc["cases"].all? { |c| c["fixture"].is_a?(String) && !c["fixture"].empty? }
    fail.("baseline_doc must record the runs it sampled: #{doc.inspect}") unless doc["runs"] == 10
    cmp = lambda do |results, bdoc, subject: subj, corpus: "c0rpus", only: nil|
      capture_io { compare_to_baseline(results, bdoc, subject: subject, corpus: corpus, only: only, agent: adm) }
    rescue StandardError => e
      [:raised, "", "#{e.class}: #{e.message}"]
    end
    certified = ->(o) { o.match?(/^baseline verdict: CERTIFIED\b/) }
    uncertified = ->(o) { o.match?(/^baseline verdict: UNCERTIFIED\b/) }

    # (a) same subject, same corpus, every case measured -> CERTIFIED, exit 0.
    c, o, e = cmp.(run2, doc)
    fail.("a fully covered same-subject same-corpus run must CERTIFY, exit 0 (got #{c.inspect}): #{o}#{e}") \
      unless c == 0 && certified.(o)

    # (b) subject mismatch -> UNCERTIFIED naming the subject, with a Fix:.
    c, o, e = cmp.(run2, doc, subject: other)
    fail.("a subject mismatch must read UNCERTIFIED, never CERTIFIED (got #{c.inspect}): #{o}#{e}") \
      unless uncertified.(o) && !certified.(o)
    fail.("a subject-mismatch verdict must name the subject and carry a Fix: #{o}#{e}") \
      unless o.include?("subject") && e.include?("Fix:")

    # (c) corpus fingerprint mismatch -> UNCERTIFIED naming the corpus.
    c, o, e = cmp.(run2, doc, corpus: "0therc0rpus")
    fail.("a corpus mismatch must read UNCERTIFIED (got #{c.inspect}): #{o}#{e}") unless uncertified.(o)
    fail.("a corpus-mismatch verdict must name the corpus and carry a Fix: #{o}#{e}") \
      unless o.include?("corpus") && e.include?("Fix:")

    # (d) a case in the corpus the baseline never measured: passing ->
    #     UNCERTIFIED naming it; FAILING -> exit 1 (nothing certifies it).
    run3 = run2 + [row.("AE-03", true, "10/10", fixture_fingerprint(f3))]
    c, o, e = cmp.(run3, doc)
    fail.("a case the baseline never measured must read UNCERTIFIED, exit 0 (got #{c.inspect}): #{o}#{e}") \
      unless c == 0 && uncertified.(o) && o.include?("AE-03")
    run3f = run2 + [row.("AE-03", false, "2/10", fixture_fingerprint(f3))]
    c, o, e = cmp.(run3f, doc)
    fail.("a FAILING case the baseline never measured must exit 1 (got #{c.inspect}): #{o}#{e}") \
      unless c == 1 && e.include?("AE-03") && e.include?("Fix:")
    # the pre-DND-522 signature shows the same hole: diff_baseline alone exited 0
    c, = capture_io { diff_baseline(run3f, load_baseline_cases(doc)) }
    fail.("diff_baseline must fail a failing case the baseline never measured (got #{c.inspect})") unless c == 1

    # (e) a case in the baseline the run lacks -> a regression, never "0 regressed".
    c, o, e = cmp.(run2.take(1), doc)
    fail.("a baseline case missing from a full run must exit 1 (got #{c.inspect}): #{o}#{e}") \
      unless c == 1 && e.include?("AE-02") && !certified.(o)

    # (f) a case whose FIXTURE changed since the baseline is not comparable:
    #     its baseline rate scored a different test.
    c, o, e = cmp.([row.("AE-01", true, "10/10", fp1b), run2[1]], doc)
    fail.("a changed fixture must read UNCERTIFIED naming the case (got #{c.inspect}): #{o}#{e}") \
      unless uncertified.(o) && o.include?("AE-01")
    c, _o, e = cmp.([row.("AE-01", false, "0/10", fp1b), run2[1]], doc)
    fail.("a FAILING case on a changed fixture must exit 1 (got #{c.inspect}): #{e}") unless c == 1

    # (g) a legacy baseline (no subject, no corpus, no per-case fixture) is
    #     UNKNOWN on both, never CERTIFIED.
    legacy = { "cases" => doc["cases"].map { |x| x.reject { |k, _| k == "fixture" } } }
    c, o, e = cmp.(run2, legacy)
    fail.("a legacy baseline must read UNCERTIFIED with subject+corpus UNKNOWN (got #{c.inspect}): #{o}#{e}") \
      unless uncertified.(o) && o.include?("subject UNKNOWN") && o.include?("corpus UNKNOWN")

    # (h) --only scopes the comparison: an unselected case is out of scope, and
    #     a scoped run over a matching subset still names itself as partial.
    c, o, e = cmp.(run2.take(1), doc, only: "AE-01")
    fail.("--only must not report unselected baseline cases as missing (got #{c.inspect}): #{o}#{e}") \
      unless c == 0
    fail.("an --only run must not read as a CERTIFIED full corpus: #{o}") if certified.(o)

    # (i) a PRESENT but unreadable baseline is an error, never "no baseline".
    bad = File.join(d, "bad.json")
    File.write(bad, "{not json")
    raised = begin
      load_baseline_doc(bad)
      nil
    rescue BaselineUnreadable => e
      e.message
    end
    fail.("an unparseable baseline must raise BaselineUnreadable with a Fix:, not read as absent") \
      unless raised&.include?("Fix:")
    fail.("an absent baseline must load as nil (no baseline committed yet)") \
      unless load_baseline_doc(File.join(d, "absent.json")).nil?

    # (j) --update-baseline must describe the WHOLE corpus at the calibrated N.
    fail.("--update-baseline with --only must be refused with a Fix:") \
      unless update_baseline_error(only: "AE-01", runs: DEFAULT_RUNS).to_s.include?("Fix:")
    fail.("--update-baseline below N=#{DEFAULT_RUNS} must be refused with a Fix:") \
      unless update_baseline_error(only: nil, runs: 3).to_s.include?("Fix:")
    fail.("--update-baseline over the full corpus at the default N must be allowed") \
      unless update_baseline_error(only: nil, runs: DEFAULT_RUNS).nil?
  end

  # -- 8. T1 hook-stdin primitive: three states, and :error is NEVER a pass ---
  # (the coordinator's fix #1: a broken/absent hook returning "no deny" must not
  # satisfy an expect=clean case — that is a dead guard reading as a working one.)
  Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-hook-") do |d|
    hooks = File.join(d, "hooks")
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(hooks)
    denier = File.join(hooks, "denier.sh")
    allower = File.join(hooks, "allower.sh")
    File.write(denier, %(#!/bin/sh\necho '{"hookSpecificOutput":{"permissionDecision":"deny"}}'\n))
    File.write(allower, "#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n")
    FileUtils.chmod(0o755, denier)
    FileUtils.chmod(0o755, allower)
    inp = File.join(d, "in.json")
    File.write(inp, "{}")

    fail.("a denying hook must verdict :fires") unless hook_verdict_at(denier, inp).first == :fires
    fail.("an allowing hook must verdict :clean") unless hook_verdict_at(allower, inp).first == :clean
    # absent / non-executable hook -> :error (NOT :clean)
    fail.("an absent hook must verdict :error") unless hook_verdict_at(File.join(hooks, "nope.sh"), inp).first == :error
    # unreadable input -> :error
    fail.("an unreadable input must verdict :error") unless hook_verdict_at(allower, File.join(d, "missing.json")).first == :error
    # a real missing guard name resolves through HOOKS_DIR to :error
    fail.("a missing guard name must verdict :error") unless hook_verdict("nonexistent-guard-zzz", inp).first == :error

    # verdict -> pass mapping: :error is never a pass, for EITHER expectation.
    fail.(":fires satisfies expect=fires") unless hook_pass?(:fires, "fires")
    fail.(":clean satisfies expect=clean") unless hook_pass?(:clean, "clean")
    fail.(":clean must NOT satisfy expect=fires") if hook_pass?(:clean, "fires")
    fail.(":error must FAIL an expect=clean case (dead guard != working allow)") if hook_pass?(:error, "clean")
    fail.(":error must FAIL an expect=fires case") if hook_pass?(:error, "fires")
  end

  # -- 8b. case-config validation (fix #2 + #4): a case must ASSERT something;
  # a structured-answer REQUIRES an answer_field; a malformed expect_order FAILs
  # (with a Fix:) instead of raising and aborting the whole run.
  # asserts nothing -> invalid
  none = score_decision({ "mode" => "next-action" }, { actions: ["wait"], answers: {} })
  fail.("a case asserting nothing must be rejected") if none[0]
  fail.("assert-nothing rejection must carry a Fix:") unless none[1].join.include?("Fix:")
  # structured-answer with forbid_action but no answer_field, empty output -> must FAIL, not vacuously pass
  sa_noanswer = score_decision({ "mode" => "structured-answer", "forbid_action" => ["slack.dm-owner"] },
                               { actions: [], answers: {} })
  fail.("structured-answer without answer_field must be rejected (would pass vacuously)") if sa_noanswer[0]
  fail.("structured-answer-without-answer_field rejection must carry a Fix:") unless sa_noanswer[1].join.include?("Fix:")
  # answer_field with no expect_answer -> invalid
  af_noexpect = score_decision({ "mode" => "structured-answer", "answer_field" => "X" },
                               { actions: [], answers: { "X" => "y" } })
  fail.("answer_field without expect_answer must be rejected") if af_noexpect[0]
  # malformed expect_order (no `<`) -> FAIL, and must NOT raise
  bad_order = nil
  begin
    bad_order = score_decision({ "mode" => "next-action", "expect_order" => ["confirm-merged then done"] },
                               { actions: ["confirm-merged"], answers: {} })
  rescue StandardError => e
    fail.("a malformed expect_order must not raise (got #{e.class}: #{e.message})")
  end
  fail.("a malformed expect_order must FAIL its case") if bad_order && bad_order[0]
  fail.("malformed expect_order must carry a Fix:") if bad_order && !bad_order[1].join.include?("Fix:")
  # validate_case directly: a well-formed next-action case is valid
  fail.("a well-formed case must validate") unless validate_case({ "mode" => "next-action", "expect_action" => ["wait"] }).empty?

  # -- 9. inert PATH sandbox: stubs are inert; git push blocked; git read
  # forwarded; and model_env prepends the stub bin WITHOUT overriding HOME (the
  # ambient HOME is required for claude auth + --agent resolution — the design
  # §2.1 deviation documented in the file header).
  Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-sb-") do |d|
    bin = build_sandbox(d)
    env = model_env(bin)
    fail.("model_env must NOT override HOME (claude needs the ambient HOME to auth + resolve --agent)") if env.key?("HOME")
    fail.("model PATH must front-load the stub bin") unless env["PATH"].start_with?("#{bin}:")
    INERT_STUBS.each do |name|
      out, st = Open3.capture2e(env, name, "whatever", "args", stdin_data: "")
      fail.("stub #{name} must be inert (nonzero exit)") if st.success?
      fail.("stub #{name} must announce INERT STUB") unless out.include?("INERT STUB")
    end
    # git read forwarded (git --version works), git push blocked.
    ver_out, ver_st = Open3.capture2e(env, "git", "--version", stdin_data: "")
    fail.("git --version must be forwarded (got: #{ver_out.inspect})") unless ver_st.success? && ver_out =~ /git version/
    push_out, push_st = Open3.capture2e(env, "git", "push", "origin", "main", stdin_data: "")
    fail.("git push must be blocked by the sandbox") if push_st.success?
    fail.("git push block must announce INERT STUB") unless push_out.include?("INERT STUB 'git push'")
    # git's own children resolve through the sandbox PATH too (DND-1103): a
    # `!` alias that calls gh hits the stub, not the real tool. (The inner
    # `git` of a `!` alias is not covered: git puts its exec-path first on the
    # child's PATH, so it reaches git-core's git, with or without this shim.)
    repo = File.join(d, "repo")
    _o, init_st = Open3.capture2e(env, "git", "init", "-q", repo, stdin_data: "")
    fail.("the sandbox git must init a fixture repo") unless init_st.success?
    [["!gh pr create", "INERT STUB 'gh'"], ["!docker ps", "INERT STUB 'docker'"]].each do |body, want|
      al_out, al_st = Open3.capture2e(env, "git", "-c", "alias.p=#{body}", "p", chdir: repo, stdin_data: "")
      fail.("a git alias running `#{body}` must still hit the sandbox stub (got: #{al_out.inspect})") \
        if al_st.success? || !al_out.include?(want)
    end
  end

  # -- 10. plan-only framing is appended for T2 modes ----------------------
  fail.("next-action framing must forbid tool use") \
    unless trailer_instructions("next-action").include?("Do NOT call any tool")
  fail.("next-action framing must ask for NEXT_ACTIONS") \
    unless trailer_instructions("next-action").include?("NEXT_ACTIONS:")
  fail.("structured-answer framing must forbid tool use") \
    unless trailer_instructions("structured-answer").include?("Do NOT call any tool")
  argv = model_argv(default_agent_file(adm))
  fail.("model argv must invoke the eval subject (claude -p --agent #{adm.subject_key})") \
    unless argv[0, 4] == ["claude", "-p", "--agent", adm.subject_key]
  # -- 10b. STRUCTURAL tool containment (guard 2, hardened — design §2.1). Assert
  # the actual containment, not merely that HOME is ambient: NO MCP server may
  # load, and Bash + Task must be disallowed. This is what closes the real
  # forge/comms/Agent surfaces that the ambient HOME re-opens.
  fail.("model argv must pass --strict-mcp-config (ignore all ambient MCP config)") \
    unless argv.include?("--strict-mcp-config")
  mci = argv.index("--mcp-config")
  mcp_ok =
    if mci && argv[mci + 1]
      begin
        JSON.parse(argv[mci + 1])["mcpServers"] == {}
      rescue StandardError
        false
      end
    else
      false
    end
  fail.("model argv must pass --mcp-config with an EMPTY mcpServers set (no MCP surface loads)") unless mcp_ok
  dti = argv.index("--disallowedTools")
  disallowed = dti ? argv[(dti + 1)..] : []
  fail.("model argv must --disallowedTools Bash (closes shell + absolute-path bypass)") \
    unless disallowed.include?("Bash")
  fail.("model argv must --disallowedTools Task (closes real captain spawn)") \
    unless disallowed.include?("Task")
  # --disallowedTools is variadic: anything after it is eaten as a tool name. So
  # it must stay LAST, carrying exactly the containment set — a subject flag
  # placed after it would silently become a "disallowed tool" instead.
  fail.("--disallowedTools must be the last flag, carrying exactly Bash Task (got #{disallowed.inspect})") \
    unless disallowed == %w[Bash Task]

  # -- 11. SUBJECT SELECTION (DND-503). `claude --agent <name>` resolves <name>
  # from ~/.claude/agents, a symlink to the MAIN checkout, whatever the cwd. So
  # a bare `--agent athena-admiral` evaluates main's admiral even when the eval
  # runs in a variant worktree, and a variant-vs-baseline T2 delta measures one
  # subject twice. The argv must carry the NAMED file's prose itself.
  Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-subject-") do |d|
    front = "---\nname: #{adm.name}\ndescription: eval subject\nmodel: opus\ncolor: red\n---\n\n"
    a = File.join(d, "a.md")
    b = File.join(d, "b.md")
    File.write(a, front + "You are variant A.\n")
    File.write(b, front + "You are variant B.\n")

    argv_a = model_argv(a)
    argv_b = model_argv(b)
    fail.("two agent files whose prose differs must yield different model argv") if argv_a == argv_b

    ai = argv_a.index("--agents")
    defs = ai && argv_a[ai + 1] ? (JSON.parse(argv_a[ai + 1]) rescue nil) : nil
    fail.("model argv must define the subject inline via --agents <json>") unless defs.is_a?(Hash)
    if defs.is_a?(Hash)
      fail.("--agents must define exactly the subject key #{adm.subject_key} (got #{defs.keys.inspect})") \
        unless defs.keys == [adm.subject_key]
      sub = defs[adm.subject_key] || {}
      fail.("the subject prompt must be the named file's body") unless sub["prompt"].to_s.include?("You are variant A.")
      fail.("the subject prompt must not carry the frontmatter") if sub["prompt"].to_s.include?("name: #{adm.name}")
      fail.("the subject must keep the file's model") unless sub["model"] == "opus"
      fail.("the subject must keep the file's description") unless sub["description"] == "eval subject"
    end
    agent_i = argv_a.index("--agent")
    fail.("--agent must name the inline subject #{adm.subject_key}") unless agent_i && argv_a[agent_i + 1] == adm.subject_key
    fail.("model argv must never pass the bare --agent #{adm.name} (resolves from the main checkout)") \
      if argv_a.each_cons(2).include?(["--agent", adm.name])
    shipped = Dir.glob(File.join(AI_DIR, "agents", "*.md")).map { |f| File.basename(f, ".md") }
    fail.("#{adm.subject_key} must be a name no agents dir supplies, so a lost --agents fails loud") \
      if shipped.include?(adm.subject_key)
    fail.("the subject fingerprint must follow the named file") \
      if subject_fingerprint(a)["sha256"] == subject_fingerprint(b)["sha256"]

    # A subject that cannot be loaded is an ERROR with a Fix:, never a silent
    # fallback to whatever the bare name resolves.
    bad = {
      "missing file" => File.join(d, "nope.md"),
      "no frontmatter" => File.join(d, "plain.md"),
      "wrong agent name" => File.join(d, "captain.md"),
      "unknown frontmatter key" => File.join(d, "unknown.md"),
      "empty body" => File.join(d, "empty.md")
    }
    File.write(bad["no frontmatter"], "You are an admiral.\n")
    File.write(bad["wrong agent name"], front.sub("name: #{adm.name}", "name: athena-captain") + "prose\n")
    File.write(bad["unknown frontmatter key"], front.sub("color: red", "permissionMode: bypass") + "prose\n")
    File.write(bad["empty body"], front)
    bad.each do |label, path|
      begin
        agent_definition(path)
        fail.("#{label}: agent_definition must raise SubjectError")
      rescue SubjectError => e
        fail.("#{label}: the SubjectError must carry a Fix:") unless e.message.include?("Fix:")
      end
    end

    # --agent-file resolution: default is THIS checkout's render; an explicit
    # path is absolutized; a flag with no value is an error, not the default.
    path, err = resolve_agent_file(%w[--run])
    fail.("no --agent-file must default to this checkout's #{default_agent_file(adm)}") unless path == default_agent_file(adm) && err.nil?
    path, err = resolve_agent_file(["--run", "--agent-file", a])
    fail.("--agent-file <path> must select that path") unless path == a && err.nil?
    _path, err = resolve_agent_file(%w[--run --agent-file])
    fail.("a valueless --agent-file must be an error with a Fix:") unless err.to_s.include?("Fix:")
    _path, err = resolve_agent_file(["--run", "--agent-file", bad["missing file"]])
    fail.("a missing --agent-file must be an error with a Fix:") unless err.to_s.include?("Fix:")
    # An over-size render must fail at RESOLUTION (the CLI's exit-2 path), not
    # as an unrescued raise from run_model.
    big = File.join(d, "big.md")
    File.write(big, front + ("x" * (MAX_SUBJECT_ARG_BYTES + 1)) + "\n")
    path, err = resolve_agent_file(["--run", "--agent-file", big])
    fail.("an over-size --agent-file must be a resolution error with a Fix:") \
      unless path.nil? && err.to_s.include?("Fix:") && err.to_s.include?("single-argument limit")
  end

  # -- DND-225: numeric score ----------------------------------------------
  # (a) regression: an invalid case config samples NOTHING (no model call), so
  # its rate is 0/0 (n/a), never "0/K" — which read as K measured failures and
  # dragged the numeric score down with a config error.
  Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-selftest-cfg-") do |d|
    fx = File.join(d, "AE-99-vacuous")
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(fx)
    File.write(File.join(fx, "meta"), "mode = next-action\ninvariant = asserts nothing\n")
    File.write(File.join(fx, "scenario.md"), "x\n")
    r = evaluate(fx, runs: 3, argv: ["claude-must-not-run"])
    fail.("an invalid case config must rate 0/0 (n/a), got #{r[:rate].inspect}") unless r[:rate] == "0/0"
    fail.("an invalid case config must still FAIL") if r[:pass]
  end
  # (b) the score block: aggregate over SAMPLED (T2) cases only; a T1 case is
  # deterministic and reported apart; a 0/0 case is n/a and never a 0.
  sb = score_lines([
    { name: "AE-01-a", mode: "next-action", pass: true, rate: "20/20" },
    { name: "AE-02-b", mode: "next-action", pass: true, rate: "10/20" },
    { name: "AE-13a-t1", mode: "hook-stdin", pass: true, rate: "1/1" },
    { name: "AE-99-bad", mode: "next-action", pass: false, rate: "0/0" }
  ], "abc123def456").join("\n")
  fail.("score block must print the T2 aggregate 0.75 over 2 sampled cases, got:\n#{sb}") \
    unless sb.include?("aggregate 0.75 over 2 sampled case(s)")
  fail.("score block must count the 0/0 case as n/a") unless sb.include?("n/a: 1")
  fail.("score block must render the 0/0 case as n/a") unless sb.match?(/AE-99-bad\s+n\/a/)
  fail.("score block must print a Wilson interval per sampled case") unless sb.include?("0.50 [0.30,0.70] (10/20)")
  fail.("score block must report T1 cases apart, as deterministic") unless sb.include?("T1 deterministic: 1/1 pass")
  fail.("score block must name the corpus fingerprint") unless sb.include?("corpus abc123def456")
  none = score_lines([{ name: "AE-99-bad", mode: "next-action", pass: false, rate: "0/0" }], "x").join("\n")
  fail.("with nothing sampled the aggregate must read n/a, never 0") unless none.include?("aggregate n/a")

  # -- DND-505: the CLI refuses an argument it does not recognise ----------
  # Black-box through the real CLI first. Every argv here is side-effect-free
  # even on a parser that ignores unknown flags (the ignored-flag path is
  # `list`), so this block can never start a model run from inside the gate.
  cli = ->(*args) { Open3.capture3(RbConfig.ruby, File.expand_path(__FILE__), *args) }
  [
    [%w[--rnus 5], "--rnus"],        # typo'd flag
    [%w[--corpus-full], "--corpus-full"], # a flag this tool never had
    [%w[--only], "--only"],          # value flag with no value
    [%w[stray], "stray"],            # positional argument
    # DND-537: an out-of-range --runs must be REFUSED, not silently clamped
    # into 1-99. --only names a fixture that matches nothing, so this can
    # never start a model run even on the old clamp-and-continue behavior.
    [%w[--run --runs 0 --only dnd-537-no-such-fixture], "--runs"],
    [%w[--run --runs 100 --only dnd-537-no-such-fixture], "--runs"]
  ].each do |args, named|
    out, err, st = cli.(*args)
    fail.("`admiral-eval #{args.join(' ')}` must exit 2 (usage), got #{st.exitstatus}") unless st.exitstatus == 2
    fail.("`admiral-eval #{args.join(' ')}` must name #{named} on stderr: #{err.inspect}") unless err.include?(named)
    fail.("`admiral-eval #{args.join(' ')}` must carry a Fix: line") unless err.include?("Fix:")
    fail.("`admiral-eval #{args.join(' ')}` must not run the default list: #{out[0, 80].inspect}") unless out.empty?
  end
  out, _err, st = cli.("--help", "--bogus")
  fail.("--help must still win over any other argument (stdout Usage, exit 0)") \
    unless st.exitstatus.zero? && out.include?("Usage:")

  # In-process: the parser behind that dispatch, including the cases the
  # black-box block cannot exercise safely (they carry --run).
  _o, reason = parse_cli(%w[--run --runs])
  fail.("--run --runs (no value) must be a usage error naming --runs") unless reason.to_s.include?("--runs")
  _o, reason = parse_cli(%w[--run --runs many])
  fail.("a non-integer --runs must be a usage error") unless reason.to_s.include?("integer")
  _o, reason = parse_cli(%w[--run --rnus 5])
  fail.("--run --rnus must be a usage error naming --rnus") unless reason.to_s.include?("--rnus")
  _o, reason = parse_cli(%w[--run --agent-file])
  fail.("--run --agent-file (no value) must be a usage error") unless reason.to_s.include?("--agent-file")
  _o, reason = parse_cli(%w[--runs 5])
  fail.("a --run option without --run must be a usage error, never an ignored flag") \
    unless reason.to_s.include?("--run")
  _o, reason = parse_cli(%w[--run --self-test])
  fail.("--run with --self-test must be a usage error") unless reason.to_s.include?("--self-test")
  _o, reason = parse_cli(%w[--self-test --only x])
  fail.("a --run option with --self-test must be a usage error") unless reason.to_s.include?("--only")
  fwd = %w[--run --runs 4 --only x --update-baseline --agent-file p.md]
  rev = %w[--agent-file p.md --update-baseline --only x --runs 4 --run]
  o1, r1 = parse_cli(fwd)
  o2, r2 = parse_cli(rev)
  fail.("well-formed --run options must parse (got #{r1.inspect} / #{r2.inspect})") unless r1.nil? && r2.nil?
  fail.("flag order must not matter: #{o1.inspect} vs #{o2.inspect}") unless o1 == o2
  fail.("--runs must parse as an Integer") unless o1 && o1["--runs"] == 4
  [[], %w[--self-test]].each do |args|
    _o, reason = parse_cli(args)
    fail.("`admiral-eval #{args.join(' ')}` must parse, got #{reason.inspect}") unless reason.nil?
  end

  # DND-521: the REAL AE-19c meta must keep its teeth. The wrong behaviour the
  # admiral showed (0/40 on 623d9db8, 0/7 on fdfbffb: drain ended with no
  # release) and a release before the final report must both FAIL; the
  # athena:fleet-drain step-5 order must PASS. Deleting or loosening either
  # expect_order turns this red.
  ae19c_meta = File.join(fixtures_dir(adm), "AE-19c-drained-after-last-return", "meta")
  if File.file?(ae19c_meta)
    m19c = parse_meta(ae19c_meta)
    s19c = ->(line) { score_decision(m19c, parse_trailer("NEXT_ACTIONS: #{line}"))[0] }
    fail.("AE-19c must FAIL a drain that ends without `fleet-resume.drained` (the DND-521 behaviour)") \
      if s19c.("invoke:admiral-final-report, fleet-report.admiral-state:drained, end-turn")
    fail.("AE-19c must FAIL a release before the final report") \
      if s19c.("fleet-report.admiral-state:drained, fleet-resume.drained:r, invoke:admiral-final-report, end-turn")
    fail.("AE-19c must FAIL an end-turn before the release") \
      if s19c.("fleet-report.admiral-state:drained, invoke:admiral-final-report, end-turn, fleet-resume.drained:r")
    fail.("AE-19c must PASS the athena:fleet-drain step-5 order") \
      unless s19c.("fleet-report.admiral-state:drained, invoke:admiral-final-report, fleet-resume.drained:r, end-turn")
  else
    fail.("AE-19c fixture meta missing at #{ae19c_meta}")
  end

  # -- DND-525: --run prints a per-case progress line as each case finishes ---
  # Before this fix a ~60min --run printed nothing until every case finished,
  # so a hung run looked the same as a slow one from outside. Real CLI
  # subprocess (not an in-process call), using the T1 AE-13a-forge-auth-deny
  # fixture so this exercises NO model call. Byte-for-byte stdout parity is the
  # part that matters most: variant-eval's collect_t2_counts (ai/bin/variant-eval)
  # parses admiral-eval's stdout (the `subject: ...` line via confirm_t2_subject!,
  # the `PASS|FAIL <name> <k>/<n> [<mode>] ...` rows via parse_t2_rows, and the
  # closing `admiral-eval: P/T cases pass` summary via T2_SUMMARY_LINE) and must
  # see it unchanged; the progress line must never appear there.
  out525, err525, = cli.("--run", "--only", "AE-13a", "--runs", "1")
  fail.("--run --only AE-13a must still print the subject line on stdout") \
    unless out525.include?("subject: ")
  fail.("--run --only AE-13a must still print the PASS/FAIL row on stdout") \
    unless out525.match?(/\A(?:.*\n)*PASS\s+AE-13a-forge-auth-deny\s+1\/1\s+\[hook-stdin\]/)
  fail.("--run --only AE-13a must still print the closing summary on stdout") \
    unless out525.match?(/^admiral-eval: 1\/1 cases pass/)
  fail.("the progress line must go to STDERR, never STDOUT (variant-eval only reads stdout)") \
    if out525.match?(/\[1\/1\]\s+AE-13a-forge-auth-deny/)
  fail.("--run must print a `[done/total] <case>: PASS|FAIL (<rate>)` progress line to stderr as the " \
        "case finishes, got stderr:\n#{err525}") \
    unless err525.match?(/^\s*\[1\/1\]\s+AE-13a-forge-auth-deny:\s+PASS\s+\(1\/1\)\s*$/)
  # T2_INVOCATION_FAILED in variant-eval matches `/^\s*! admiral invocation failed/` —
  # the progress line must never collide with it (would misattribute a healthy
  # run's progress line as a failed model invocation).
  fail.("the progress line must not collide with variant-eval's T2_INVOCATION_FAILED pattern") \
    if err525.match?(/^\s*! admiral invocation failed/)

  # -- DND-529: one runner, several agents --------------------------------
  # (a) the vocabulary table and the registry agree: every agent this runner
  # measures has a vocabulary, and no vocabulary names an agent it does not.
  keys = EvalSubject.keys_for_runner(RUNNER)
  fail.("VOCABULARIES keys #{VOCABULARIES.keys.sort.inspect} must equal the registry's #{keys.inspect}") \
    unless VOCABULARIES.keys.sort == keys
  # (b) every agent's corpus is lint-clean (invariant line, known mode, valid
  # config, only tokens its vocabulary offers, examples that agree with the scorer).
  keys.each do |k|
    corpus_problems(eval_agent(k)).each { |p| fail.("corpus #{k}: #{p}") }
  end
  # (c) the lint itself has teeth: a vacuous forbid on an unoffered token, a
  # missing invariant line, and a mislabeled example must each be caught.
  Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-lint-") do |root|
    fake = EvalSubject::Agent.new(key: "captain", name: "athena-captain", runner: RUNNER,
                                  fixtures_rel: File.join(root, "fx"), baseline_rel: File.join(root, "b.json"))
    fx = File.join(root, "fx", "CE-99-bad")
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(fx)
    File.write(File.join(fx, "meta"), "mode = next-action\nexpect_action = write-report\nforbid_action = fleet-control.check\n")
    File.write(File.join(fx, "scenario.md"), "x\n")
    File.write(File.join(fx, "examples"), "pass | NEXT_ACTIONS: end-turn\nfail | NEXT_ACTIONS: write-report\n")
    lint = corpus_problems(fake).join("\n")
    fail.("lint must flag a token the agent's vocabulary never offers:\n#{lint}") unless lint.include?("fleet-control.check")
    fail.("lint must flag a case with no invariant line") unless lint.include?("invariant")
    fail.("lint must flag an example the scorer disagrees with") unless lint.include?("labeled pass but the scorer says fail")
    empty = EvalSubject::Agent.new(key: "captain", name: "athena-captain", runner: RUNNER,
                                   fixtures_rel: File.join(root, "none"), baseline_rel: "x")
    fail.("lint must flag an agent with zero cases") unless corpus_problems(empty).join.include?("zero cases")
  end
  # (d) each agent resolves to its OWN render, corpus and vocabulary.
  keys.each do |k|
    ag = eval_agent(k)
    path, err = resolve_agent_file(%w[--run], ag)
    fail.("#{k}: no --agent-file must default to #{ag.render_rel}") unless err.nil? && path == default_agent_file(ag)
    fail.("#{k}: fixtures must come from #{ag.fixtures_rel}") \
      unless fixtures(ag).all? { |d| d.start_with?(File.join(REPO_ROOT, ag.fixtures_rel) + "/") }
    fail.("#{k}: the model argv must name #{ag.subject_key}") unless model_argv(path, ag)[3] == ag.subject_key
  end
  cap_txt = trailer_instructions("next-action", vocabulary(eval_agent("captain")))
  fail.("the captain's framing must offer the captain vocabulary") unless cap_txt.include?("sendmessage.architect")
  fail.("the captain's framing must not offer admiral-only tokens") if cap_txt.include?("fleet-control.check")
  # (e) the CLI: an unknown agent, or one another runner measures, exits 2 with
  # a Fix: and runs nothing; a known agent lists its own corpus.
  [[%w[--agent nope], "nope"], [%w[--agent diff-critic], "ai/bin/critic-eval"], [%w[--agent], "--agent"],
   [%w[--self-test --agent captain], "--agent"]].each do |args, named|
    out, err, st = cli.(*args)
    fail.("`admiral-eval #{args.join(' ')}` must exit 2 naming #{named} with a Fix:, got #{st.exitstatus} #{err[0, 160].inspect}") \
      unless st.exitstatus == 2 && err.include?(named) && err.include?("Fix:") && out.empty?
  end
  keys.each do |k|
    out, _err, st = cli.("--agent", k)
    fail.("`admiral-eval --agent #{k}` must list #{eval_agent(k).name}'s corpus (exit #{st.exitstatus})") \
      unless st.exitstatus.zero? && out.include?(eval_agent(k).name)
  end
  o1, r1 = parse_cli(%w[--run --agent captain --runs 2])
  o2, r2 = parse_cli(%w[--runs 2 --agent captain --run])
  fail.("--agent must parse with --run in any order") unless r1.nil? && r2.nil? && o1 == o2 && o1["--agent"] == "captain"
  # -- DND-524: usage/cost capture — a call reporting usage aggregates; a call
  # reporting none reads n/a, never a spend of 0 (the failed-lookup-must-not-
  # look-like-an-empty-one rule). Unit-level first (extract_usage /
  # usage_summary_line, no subprocess), then one CLI-level probe with a
  # stubbed `claude` on PATH so the whole model_argv -> invoke_subject ->
  # run_model wiring is proven end to end without a real model call in the
  # gate.
  #
  # (critic finding on ffd8309): the stubbed `claude` below ignores its argv
  # and always answers in the --output-format json shape, so the CLI probe
  # alone can't tell "model_argv passes --output-format json" from "it
  # doesn't" — a regression there would stay green there and only break a REAL
  # claude call (plain text, so every real case would JSON::ParserError and
  # FAIL). Assert the flag directly on the built argv.
  fail.("model_argv must pass --output-format json (else a real claude call answers in plain text, " \
        "which the stubbed self-test claude above can't catch since it ignores its argv)") \
    unless model_argv(default_agent_file).each_cons(2).include?(%w[--output-format json])
  fail.("extract_usage must read the --output-format json usage/cost fields") \
    unless extract_usage({ "usage" => { "input_tokens" => 5, "output_tokens" => 7,
                                         "cache_read_input_tokens" => 9, "cache_creation_input_tokens" => 11 },
                            "total_cost_usd" => 0.25 }) ==
           { "input_tokens" => 5, "output_tokens" => 7, "cache_read_input_tokens" => 9,
             "cache_creation_input_tokens" => 11, "cost_usd" => 0.25 }
  fail.("extract_usage must be nil when `usage` is absent (never a zero-filled hash)") \
    unless extract_usage({ "result" => "ok" }).nil?
  fail.("extract_usage must be nil when `usage` is not a Hash") \
    unless extract_usage({ "usage" => nil }).nil?

  fail.("usage_summary_line must aggregate measured calls") \
    unless usage_summary_line([
      { "input_tokens" => 1, "output_tokens" => 2, "cache_read_input_tokens" => 3,
        "cache_creation_input_tokens" => 4, "cost_usd" => 0.1 },
      { "input_tokens" => 10, "output_tokens" => 20, "cache_read_input_tokens" => 30,
        "cache_creation_input_tokens" => 40, "cost_usd" => 0.2 }
    ]).match?(/2\/2 model call\(s\).*input 11.*output 22.*cache_read 33.*cache_creation 44.*cost \$0\.3000 USD/)
  fail.("usage_summary_line must read n/a when NOTHING was measured, never 0 (DND-524)") \
    unless usage_summary_line([nil, nil]) == "admiral-eval: usage — n/a (no model call in this run reported usage tokens)"
  fail.("usage_summary_line must read n/a on an empty log too") \
    unless usage_summary_line([]).include?("n/a")
  fail.("usage_summary_line's n/a line must never contain a bare '0' spend/token reading") \
    if usage_summary_line([nil]) =~ /\$0\.00|tokens: input 0/

  # CLI-level: a stubbed `claude` ahead of the real one on PATH, run through the
  # real entry point (model_argv -> invoke_subject -> run_model), against a T2
  # (next-action) fixture so invoke_subject's JSON parse path actually runs.
  Dir.mktmpdir("admiral-eval-usage-cli-") do |d|
    stub_bin = File.join(d, "bin")
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(stub_bin)
    agent_file = File.join(d, "athena-admiral.md")
    File.write(agent_file, "---\nname: athena-admiral\ndescription: eval subject\nmodel: opus\ncolor: red\n---\n\n" \
                            "You are a stub admiral.\n")
    env = { "PATH" => "#{stub_bin}:#{ENV['PATH']}" }

    # (a) usage present -> aggregated, not n/a.
    File.write(File.join(stub_bin, "claude"), <<~SH)
      #!/bin/sh
      cat <<'JSON'
      {"result":"NEXT_ACTIONS: wait","usage":{"input_tokens":11,"output_tokens":22,"cache_read_input_tokens":33,"cache_creation_input_tokens":44},"total_cost_usd":0.0123}
      JSON
    SH
    FileUtils.chmod(0o755, File.join(stub_bin, "claude"))
    out_a, err_a, = Open3.capture3(env, RbConfig.ruby, File.expand_path(__FILE__),
                                    "--run", "--only", "AE-01-confirm-before-done", "--runs", "1",
                                    "--agent-file", agent_file)
    fail.("stdout must be unaffected by --output-format json / usage capture") \
      unless out_a.match?(/^subject: /) && out_a.match?(/^admiral-eval: \d+\/\d+ cases pass/)
    fail.("the usage line must never appear on stdout (byte-parity for variant-eval)") \
      if out_a.include?("usage —")
    expected_a = usage_summary_line([{ "input_tokens" => 11, "output_tokens" => 22,
                                        "cache_read_input_tokens" => 33, "cache_creation_input_tokens" => 44,
                                        "cost_usd" => 0.0123 }])
    fail.("a measured call must report its usage on stderr, got:\n#{err_a}") \
      unless err_a.include?(expected_a)

    # (b) usage absent from an otherwise-successful, well-formed call -> n/a,
    # never 0. This is the n/a path (a `claude` too old to emit `usage` under
    # --output-format json, or one that omits it for some other reason) — NOT
    # a reproduction of the pre-fix code, which never asked for
    # --output-format json at all and so got plain text, not a JSON envelope
    # missing one field (critic finding on ffd8309: the real regression
    # evidence for that is in the commit message, not this fixture).
    File.write(File.join(stub_bin, "claude"), <<~SH)
      #!/bin/sh
      cat <<'JSON'
      {"result":"NEXT_ACTIONS: wait"}
      JSON
    SH
    FileUtils.chmod(0o755, File.join(stub_bin, "claude"))
    _out_b, err_b, = Open3.capture3(env, RbConfig.ruby, File.expand_path(__FILE__),
                                     "--run", "--only", "AE-01-confirm-before-done", "--runs", "1",
                                     "--agent-file", agent_file)
    fail.("a call with no `usage` field must read n/a, not 0, got:\n#{err_b}") \
      unless err_b.include?("admiral-eval: usage — n/a (no model call in this run reported usage tokens)")

    # (c) a --output-format json envelope with NO `result` text (exit 0) is a
    # CALL failure, not a scored subject miss (critic finding on ffd8309): the
    # stderr line must carry variant-eval's exact `! admiral invocation
    # failed` prefix (T2_INVOCATION_FAILED = /^\s*! admiral invocation
    # failed/), never "invocation succeeded" — a mismatch there would make
    # collect_t2_counts count this as a real admiral miss instead of a failed
    # model call.
    File.write(File.join(stub_bin, "claude"), <<~SH)
      #!/bin/sh
      cat <<'JSON'
      {"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1,"cache_read_input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1},"total_cost_usd":0.001}
      JSON
    SH
    FileUtils.chmod(0o755, File.join(stub_bin, "claude"))
    out_c, err_c, = Open3.capture3(env, RbConfig.ruby, File.expand_path(__FILE__),
                                    "--run", "--only", "AE-01-confirm-before-done", "--runs", "1",
                                    "--agent-file", agent_file)
    fail.("a --output-format json envelope with no `result` text must read as an invocation failure " \
          "(variant-eval's T2_INVOCATION_FAILED pattern), got stderr:\n#{err_c}") \
      unless err_c.match?(/^\s*! admiral invocation failed/)
    fail.("that stderr line must never say 'invocation succeeded' (critic finding on ffd8309)") \
      if err_c.include?("invocation succeeded")
    fail.("the case must score as an invocation failure, not a subject FAIL, got stdout:\n#{out_c}") \
      unless out_c.include?("produced no output (invocation failed)")
  end

  if ok
    puts "admiral-eval: self-test OK"
    0
  else
    warn "admiral-eval: self-test FAILED"
    warn "  Fix: the scorer/parser/sampling/baseline/T1/sandbox logic is broken. A missing decision must " \
         "fail; a forbidden action present must fail; an out-of-order, one-sided, or malformed order must " \
         "fail (never raise); a case that asserts nothing (or a structured-answer with no answer_field) " \
         "must be rejected; a broken/absent hook must verdict :error and never satisfy an expect=clean " \
         "case; a baseline pass->fail must exit 1; and the inert sandbox must neutralize the PATH-resolved " \
         "forge/comms/container stubs AND block `git push`. The model argv must carry the --agent-file " \
         "subject's prose inline (--agents under #{adm.subject_key}, never a bare --agent #{adm.name}), with " \
         "--disallowedTools Bash Task last, and an unloadable subject must raise SubjectError with a Fix:. An " \
         "unknown flag, a stray argument, or a value flag with no value must exit 2 naming it (DND-505). The " \
         "AE-19c meta must fail a drain with no/late `fleet-resume.drained` and pass the fleet-drain step-5 " \
         "order: restore its two expect_order lines rather than loosening them (DND-521). Per agent (DND-529): " \
         "VOCABULARIES must cover exactly the agents EvalSubject::AGENTS assigns to #{RUNNER}; every case in " \
         "every agent's corpus needs an `invariant =` line, only tokens its agent's vocabulary offers, and " \
         "(for every agent but the admiral) an `examples` file whose pass/fail lines the scorer agrees with — " \
         "fix the case, never the example, when they disagree; each agent must default to its own render " \
         "and corpus; and `--agent` with an unknown agent, or one another runner measures, must exit 2 with a Fix:."
    1
  end
end

# Capture $stdout/$stderr of a block; return [return_value, out, err].
require "stringio"
def capture_io
  old_out, old_err = $stdout, $stderr
  o, e = StringIO.new, StringIO.new
  $stdout, $stderr = o, e
  rv = yield
  [rv, o.string, e.string]
ensure
  $stdout, $stderr = old_out, old_err
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DND-505: every argument must be one this tool declares. The old ad-hoc
# `ARGV.index` lookup ignored anything else, so `--rnus 5`, `--corpus-full`, or
# a valueless `--only` fell through to `list` and exited 0.
CLI_SPEC = {
  "--run" => :switch,
  "--self-test" => :switch,
  "--runs" => :integer,
  "--only" => :value,
  "--update-baseline" => :switch,
  "--agent-file" => :value,
  "--agent" => :value
}.freeze
RUN_OPTIONS = %w[--runs --only --update-baseline --agent-file].freeze

# -> [opts, nil] on a well-formed argv, else [nil, reason naming the argument].
def parse_cli(argv)
  opts = StrictArgv.parse(argv, CLI_SPEC)
  return [nil, "--run and --self-test are separate modes; pass one"] if opts["--run"] && opts["--self-test"]

  # --agent picks the subject for the list AND for --run; the self-test covers
  # every agent, so naming one there would be ignored — refused instead.
  return [nil, "--agent does not apply to --self-test (it checks every agent)"] if opts["--self-test"] && opts.key?("--agent")

  stray = RUN_OPTIONS.find { |f| opts.key?(f) }
  return [nil, "#{stray} only applies to --run"] if stray && !opts["--run"]

  [opts, nil]
rescue StrictArgv::UsageError => e
  [nil, e.message]
end

USAGE = <<~TXT
  Usage:
    ai/bin/admiral-eval [--agent NAME]     list an agent's behavioral-eval corpus
    ai/bin/admiral-eval --run [OPTIONS]    run the eval (MODEL-IN-LOOP: slow;
                                           NOT part of the gate)
    ai/bin/admiral-eval --self-test        deterministic suite (no model)
    ai/bin/admiral-eval --help             this text

  --agent NAME          the agent to evaluate: #{EvalSubject.keys_for_runner(RUNNER).join(', ')}
                        (default #{DEFAULT_AGENT}; the full athena-<name> also works).
                        Each has its own corpus, baseline and action vocabulary.
                        diff-critic is measured by ai/bin/critic-eval. An unknown
                        agent exits 2, never zero cases.

  Options (with --run):
    --runs N            samples per T2 case (#{RUNS_RANGE.first}-#{RUNS_RANGE.last}, default #{DEFAULT_RUNS}).
                        The row's PASS/FAIL is the majority; the baseline
                        diff classifies the counts (EvalScore.classify),
                        calibrated at the default N.
    --only NAME         restrict to one case
    --update-baseline   rewrite the baseline from this run's results (the
                        full corpus at --runs >= #{DEFAULT_RUNS}: --only or a
                        smaller --runs is refused, exit 2)
    --agent-file PATH   the rendered agent definition to evaluate
                        (default: this checkout's ai/agents/athena-<agent>.md).
                        Its prose is passed inline, so the run never falls back
                        to the ~/.claude/agents copy (the main checkout's).

  An unrecognised argument, or a value flag with no value, exits 2.
TXT

def main(argv)
  # --help is answered before anything else, whatever else is on the line.
  if argv.include?("--help") || argv.include?("-h")
    puts USAGE
    return 0
  end

  opts, reason = parse_cli(argv)
  if reason
    warn "admiral-eval: usage error — #{reason}."
    warn "  Fix: re-run with only the flags `ai/bin/admiral-eval --help` lists, each value flag followed by " \
         "its value. An unrecognised argument is refused, not ignored: ignoring it would run a different " \
         "mode (the default list) and exit 0."
    return 2
  end

  return self_test if opts["--self-test"]

  agent = begin
    eval_agent(opts.fetch("--agent", DEFAULT_AGENT))
  rescue EvalSubject::UnknownAgent => e
    warn "admiral-eval: usage error — #{e.message}"
    return 2
  end
  return list(agent) unless opts["--run"]

  runs = opts.fetch("--runs", DEFAULT_RUNS)
  unless RUNS_RANGE.cover?(runs)
    warn "admiral-eval: usage error — --runs #{runs} is out of range."
    warn "  Fix: pass --runs N where N is #{RUNS_RANGE.first}-#{RUNS_RANGE.last} (default: #{DEFAULT_RUNS}); " \
         "an out-of-range value is refused, never clamped (DND-537) — clamping would sample a different " \
         "depth than requested with no sign anything changed."
    return 2
  end

  update = opts.fetch("--update-baseline", false)
  if update && (why = update_baseline_error(only: opts["--only"], runs: runs))
    warn "admiral-eval: usage error — #{why}"
    return 2
  end

  agent_file, subject_err = resolve_agent_file(argv, agent)
  if subject_err
    warn "admiral-eval: FAILED — #{subject_err}"
    return 2
  end
  run_model(
    runs: runs,
    only: opts["--only"],
    update: update,
    agent: agent,
    agent_file: agent_file
  )
end

exit main(ARGV)
